items/ex-cm-rotating-hoop-reduced-energy.md:105:[F4] Calculus rules for the energy: sums and products of differentiable functions are differentiable ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]), and a differentiable function on an interval whose derivative vanishes identically is constant ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]], [[def-interval]]).
items/ex-cm-rotating-hoop-reduced-energy.md:127:3.1 By steps 1.1 and 2.1, $K+U_g=\frac12ma^2(\theta'^2+\Omega^2\sin^2\theta)+mga(1-\cos\theta)=E(\theta,\theta')+ma^2\Omega^2\sin^2\theta+mga$; along a solution, by [F4] and step 1.3, $$\frac{d}{dt}(K+U_g)=\frac{d}{dt}\bigl(ma^2\Omega^2\sin^2\theta\bigr)=2ma^2\Omega^2\sin\theta\cos\theta\,\theta' .$$ The phase field $(\theta,v)\mapsto(v,\Omega^2\sin\theta\cos\theta-(g/a)\sin\theta)$ is $C^1$ on $\mathbb R^2$, hence locally Lipschitz ([[lem-a-c1-map-is-locally-lipschitz-on-compact-coordinate-subsets]]); [[thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness]] supplies a solution through $(\pi/4,1)$. If $\Omega\ne0$ the displayed derivative is nonzero at the initial time of this solution, where it equals $ma^2\Omega^2$, so $K+U_g$ is not conserved along all solutions; as functions on the full phase space, $K+U_g$ agrees with $E$ up to the constant $mga$ precisely when the term $ma^2\Omega^2\sin^2\theta$ is absent, i.e. when $\Omega=0$. This is the second assertion of part 3. [F1, F4, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]
items/lem-em-smooth-forced-maxwell-cauchy-system.md:93:[F11] A continuous function on an interval with zero derivative at every interior point is constant ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]).
items/lem-em-smooth-forced-maxwell-cauchy-system.md:107:4.1 Uniqueness and the forced wave problems. Let $(\tilde E,\tilde B)$ be any second $C^\infty$ solution of the first-order system on the slab in the extension sense with the same data. Taking divergence directly in its first-order equations gives $\partial_t(\operatorname{div}\tilde E-\rho/\varepsilon)=-(\operatorname{div}J+\rho_t)/\varepsilon=0$ and $\partial_t\operatorname{div}\tilde B=-\operatorname{div}\operatorname{curl}\tilde E=0$ by [F3] and the continuity equation. For each fixed spatial point these defects have zero derivative, hence are constant in time by [F11], and their initial values vanish; thus $\operatorname{div}\tilde E=\rho/\varepsilon$ and $\operatorname{div}\tilde B=0$. Differentiating $\tilde E_t=c^2\operatorname{curl}\tilde B-J/\varepsilon$ in time and using $\operatorname{curl}\operatorname{curl}=\nabla\operatorname{div}-\Delta$ gives $c^{-2}\tilde E_{tt}=-\operatorname{curl}\operatorname{curl}\tilde E-\mu J_t$, hence $W\tilde E=-\nabla\operatorname{div}\tilde E-\mu J_t=-\nabla\rho/\varepsilon-\mu J_t$, while $W\tilde B=\mu\operatorname{curl}J$. Its initial data and initial time derivatives coincide with those used in step 1.1 (the derivative values are determined by the system and the data: $\tilde E_t(\cdot,t_0)=c^2\operatorname{curl}B_0-J(\cdot,t_0)/\varepsilon$ and $\tilde B_t(\cdot,t_0)=-\operatorname{curl}E_0$). By the uniqueness clause of [F1] applied componentwise, $\tilde E=E$ and $\tilde B=B$. [step 1.1, step 3.1, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, F11]
