items/pthm-cm08-boost-boundary.md:130:[F6] Calculus ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-interval]], [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]): sums, scalar multiples and pairings differentiate by the usual rules; a $C^1$ function on an interval is constant exactly when its derivative vanishes. For $C^2$ $U$, differentiating the vanishing directional derivative gives $\nabla_x(\mathbf a\cdot\nabla_xU)=0$, so the spatial gradient is constant along every translation segment contained in $Q$. Global finite invariance likewise gives equality of the endpoint gradients by differentiating its identity on an open neighbourhood of the two admissible endpoints.
items/thm-sr-smooth-local-herglotz-noether.md:142:1.4 Converse, the vorticity dichotomy: in inertial coordinates the Killing equation is $\partial_aK_b+\partial_bK_a=0$. Differentiating it with cyclically permuted indices and using commuting smooth mixed derivatives gives $2\partial_a\partial_bK_c=0$. Thus $\partial_aK_b=L_{ab}$ is constant on the connected open set $O$, $L_{ab}=-L_{ba}$, and $K$ is affine there. Indeed a function with zero first derivatives is locally constant by integration on coordinate segments, and locally constant functions are constant on a connected set; applying this first to $\partial_aK_b$ and then to $K_b-L_{ab}x^a$ proves the assertion. For $n=\varphi K$, $K^\flat=\varphi^{-1}n^\flat$; projecting $dn^\flat=d\varphi\wedge K^\flat+\varphi\,dK^\flat$ horizontally therefore gives $\omega=\varphi\,L|_{n^\perp}$. [F1, F2, F10, algebra]
items/thm-nrqmx-quadratic-egorov.md:118:[F4] For either self-adjoint operator $H$ in [A2], set $Y=D(H)$ with $\|u\|_Y=(\|u\|_2^2+\|Hu\|_2^2)^{1/2}$. Self-adjointness makes $H$ closed, so its graph is closed in $L^2\times L^2$ and $Y$ is Hilbert; the inclusion $Y\hookrightarrow L^2$ is continuous by $\|u\|_2\le\|u\|_Y$. The constant family $A(t)=H/\epsilon$ is self-adjoint on $Y$ and is a $C^1$ map $Y\to L^2$ with derivative zero. The common-domain evolution theorem therefore supplies a strongly continuous unitary propagator preserving $Y$ on every bounded time interval; for a constant generator its group law and inverses define the unitary group for all real times ([[thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-common-domain-nonautonomous-evolution]], [[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]]).
items/thm-nrqmx-quadratic-egorov.md:144:5.1 For the exact quadratic branch, the series defining the matrix exponential and its differentiated series converge uniformly on compact time intervals. Differentiating the affine formula in the Statement shows that it solves $\dot z=J(Gz+\ell)$ with initial value $z$. The difference $w$ of any two solutions with the same initial value satisfies $|w(t)|\le\|JG\|\int_0^{|t|}|w(s)|\,ds$, so Gronwall gives uniqueness. Uniqueness for this autonomous system gives $\Phi_t\circ\Phi_s=\Phi_{t+s}$ and $\Phi_t^{-1}=\Phi_{-t}$. Now put $A=\operatorname{Op}^W_\epsilon(b)$ for $b\in\mathcal S(\mathbb R^{2d})$. By [F9], its kernel $K_b(x,y)$ is Schwartz. Consequently $A$ maps $\mathcal S$ into itself, by differentiating the kernel integral and using the rapid decay of $K_b$ and its derivatives. With $q=(x+y)/2$ and $r=x-y$, the kernel definition gives $x_j=q_j+r_j/2$, $y_j=q_j-r_j/2$, and $\partial_x=\tfrac12\partial_q+\partial_r$, $\partial_y=\tfrac12\partial_q-\partial_r$. Integration by parts [F8] in $\xi_j$ gives $r_jK_b=i\epsilon K_{\partial_{\xi_j}b}$, and differentiation in $r_j$ gives $-i\epsilon\partial_{r_j}K_b=K_{\xi_jb}$. These identities yield, for every $1\le j\le2d$, $ \widehat z_j\operatorname{Op}^W_\epsilon(b)=\operatorname{Op}^W_\epsilon(z_jb)+\frac{i\epsilon}{2}\sum_kJ_{jk}\operatorname{Op}^W_\epsilon(\partial_kb),\qquad \operatorname{Op}^W_\epsilon(b)\widehat z_j=\operatorname{Op}^W_\epsilon(z_jb)-\frac{i\epsilon}{2}\sum_kJ_{jk}\operatorname{Op}^W_\epsilon(\partial_kb). $ Subtracting gives $[\widehat z_j,A]=i\epsilon\sum_kJ_{jk}\operatorname{Op}^W_\epsilon(\partial_kb)$. Apply this formula and the product commutator rule to the quadratic part of $H_{\epsilon,h}^0$; after combining equal terms by $G_{jk}=G_{kj}$, the result before using the left/right multiplication identities is $ \begin{aligned} [H_{\epsilon,h}^0,A] &=i\epsilon\sum_{j,k}\ell_jJ_{jk}\operatorname{Op}^W_\epsilon(\partial_kb)\\ &\quad+\frac{i\epsilon}{2}\sum_{j,k,\ell}G_{jk}\left(J_{k\ell}\widehat z_j\operatorname{Op}^W_\epsilon(\partial_\ell b)+J_{j\ell}\operatorname{Op}^W_\epsilon(\partial_\ell b)\widehat z_k\right). \end{aligned} $ Inserting the two multiplication identities above, the second-derivative contribution is proportional to $ \sum_{j,k,\ell,m}G_{jk}\left(J_{k\ell}J_{jm}-J_{j\ell}J_{km}\right)\operatorname{Op}^W_\epsilon(\partial_m\partial_\ell b), $ which is zero after swapping $j$ and $k$ in its second term. The first-derivative terms combine to $ i\epsilon\operatorname{Op}^W_\epsilon\left(\sum_{j,k,\ell}G_{jk}J_{k\ell}z_j\partial_\ell b+\sum_{j,k}\ell_jJ_{jk}\partial_kb\right)=-i\epsilon\operatorname{Op}^W_\epsilon\bigl(\nabla b\cdot J(Gz+\ell)\bigr)=-i\epsilon\operatorname{Op}^W_\epsilon(\{b,h\}). $ Here $\{b,h\}=\nabla b\cdot J\nabla h$ uses the sign convention of the Statement, and $\{b,h\}\in\mathcal S$ because $h$ is quadratic and $b$ is Schwartz. Both $A$ and $\operatorname{Op}(\{b,h\})$ are bounded by [[lem-nrqmx-weyl-hilbert-schmidt-bound]]. Since $A$ maps $\mathcal S$ to itself, the identity holds there. For each $u\in D(H_{\epsilon,h})$, graph-core density [A2] supplies $u_n\in\mathcal S$ converging to $u$ in the graph norm. As in step 1.2, boundedness of $A$ and of the commutator, followed by closedness of $H_{\epsilon,h}$, extends the identity to $D(H_{\epsilon,h})$. For fixed $t\in\mathbb R$, put $b_s=a\circ\Phi_{t-s}$ on the interval with endpoints $0,t$. The affine formula for $\Phi$ has an invertible linear part, so $b_s\in\mathcal S$: the chain rule expresses each derivative as a finite linear combination of derivatives of $a$ evaluated at $\Phi_{t-s}(z)$, and the inverse affine maps bound each polynomial weight in $z$ by a constant times the corresponding weight at $\Phi_{t-s}(z)$ on this compact time interval. These estimates also show $s\mapsto b_s$ is continuously differentiable in Schwartz seminorms. The flow law and chain rule give $\partial_sb_s=-\{b_s,h\}$. By the Hilbert–Schmidt bound, $s\mapsto\operatorname{Op}^W_\epsilon(b_s)$ is continuously differentiable in operator norm. For $\psi\in D(H_{\epsilon,h})$, differentiate $ F(s)\psi:=U_{\epsilon,h}(s)^*\operatorname{Op}^W_\epsilon(b_s)U_{\epsilon,h}(s)\psi. $ The propagator preserves the operator domain; the commutator identity just proved makes the Hamiltonian terms equal $U^*\operatorname{Op}(\{b_s,h\})U$, which cancels the symbol derivative $-U^*\operatorname{Op}(\{b_s,h\})U$. Thus $F'(s)\psi=0$, a continuous derivative extending to the endpoints. Integrating by [F7] on the interval between $0$ and $t$ gives $U_{\epsilon,h}(t)^*\operatorname{Op}(a)U_{\epsilon,h}(t)\psi=\operatorname{Op}(a\circ\Phi_t)\psi$. The domain is dense and both endpoint operators are bounded, so the identity extends to all of $L^2$. [A2, F4, F7, F8, F9, algebra] ∎
items/ex-em-rl-time-response.md:51:In particular $I(t)-V_0/R=(I_0-V_0/R)\exp(-R(t-t_0)/L)$ and hence $|I(t)-V_0/R|=|I_0-V_0/R|\exp(-R(t-t_0)/L)$ for every $t\in J$, with time constant $\tau=L/R$; $I\equiv V_0/R$ is the unique stationary solution (an extension with zero derivative, also in the singleton convention); if $J$ is unbounded above, then $I(t)\to V_0/R$ as $t\to+\infty$ along $J$; and the transient part is the free decay $\exp(-R(t-t_0)/L)$ of the initial deviation $I_0-V_0/R$. For $J=\{t_0\}$ the restriction is $I(t_0)=I_0$ and the jets are those of the same formula read through the stated extension, giving $\dot I(t_0)=(V_0-RI_0)/L$. The computation is conditional on the adopted lumped model, constant elements and fixed geometry; no measurement is involved.
items/ex-cm17-channel-flow-and-no-slip-check.md:79:[F4] Mean value theorem ([[cor-mean-value-theorem]]): if a real function is continuous on a compact interval and differentiable in its interior, its increment equals its derivative at some interior point times the interval length. In particular a function with zero derivative is constant on each compact subinterval of its open interval of definition.
items/thm-qftx-green-hyperbolic-existence.md:84:3.1 (Continuity in the actual topologies.) Fix compact input support $K$ and compact output set $L$ with a specified derivative order. Choose $a<\min t(K)$ and a finite slab containing $S_a$ and $L$; if $K$ is empty the restriction is zero. Step 1.1 constructs every $E_+f$ for $\operatorname{supp}f\subseteq K$ from zero data on this same $S_a$. The estimate in [F1] therefore bounds the selected output seminorm by a constant times finitely many fixed-$K$ input derivative seminorms. This proves continuity of $E_+$ restricted to each fixed-compact test space. The LF topology in [F2] is the locally convex inductive-limit topology of these spaces, so a linear map into the locally convex space $C^\infty(M)$ is continuous precisely when all such restrictions are continuous. Thus $E_+$ is continuous; the late-slice estimate gives continuity of $E_-$. No sequential-continuity substitute or unsupported closed-graph argument is used. [step 1.1, step 2.1, F1, F2, algebra]
items/prop-gr-fx-killing-tensor-conservation.md:70:[F4] [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]: a differentiable function on an interval whose derivative vanishes everywhere is constant.
items/thm-qft-finite-euclidean-polynomial-measure.md:95:1.2 Fix $r\ge0$. For $s=0,1,\ldots,r$, [F6] and [F7] give the pointwise derivative $(-1)^sP(q)U(q)^s\exp(-\lambda U(q))$ and its next derivative $(-1)^{s+1}P(q)U(q)^{s+1}\exp(-\lambda U(q))$. If $\lambda_0>0$ and $|h|<\lambda_0/2$, [F8] bounds the difference quotient of the $s$th derivative by $|P(q)|U(q)^{s+1}\exp(-\lambda_0U(q)/2)$, since every intermediate parameter is at least $\lambda_0/2$ and [F5] makes the exponential increasing. Put $x=U(q)$. If $x=0$ the scalar factor is zero; if $x>0$, put $y=\lambda_0x/4$. The power-series estimate in [F5] at $2y$ gives $\exp(2y)\ge(2y)^{s+1}/(s+1)!$, so its reciprocal gives $x^{s+1}\exp(-\lambda_0x/2)\le(2/\lambda_0)^{s+1}(s+1)!.$ Thus these difference quotients are bounded by a finite constant times $|P(q)|$, integrable by [F2]. [F3] permits differentiation under the integral inductively through order $r$, giving $N_P^{(r)}(\lambda)=(-1)^r\int P U^r\exp(-\lambda U)\,d\gamma_C$ for $\lambda>0$. Taking $P=1$ gives the formula for $Z$. [F2, F3, F5, F6, F7, F8, algebra]
