items/lem-gr-warped-base-vacuum-reduction.md:120:[L3] The exterior derivative of a smooth function is the covector of its partial derivatives; the chain, product and quotient rules hold, and a smooth function on a connected nonempty open Euclidean set whose differential is zero is constant ([[thm-zero-derivative-on-connected-open-euclidean-set-iff-constant]], with $m=2$, $q=1$; [[def-derivative]], [[thm-chain-rule]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds]], [[def-interval]]).
items/lem-em-harmonic-forcing-periodic-response.md:70:[F5] Zero-derivative corollary ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]): if $J\subseteq\mathbb R$ is order-convex ([[def-interval]]), $f:J\to\mathbb R$ is continuous on $J$ and differentiable at every interior point of $J$ with $f'=0$ there, then $f$ is constant on $J$; if $f,g:J\to\mathbb R$ are continuous on $J$ and differentiable at every interior point of $J$ with $f'=g'$ there, then $f-g$ is constant on $J$. The interior of $\mathbb R$ is $\mathbb R$, the interior of every nonempty open interval is itself, and every point of a nondegenerate interval that lies in its interior is an interior point ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-r]], [[def-interval]]).
