items/ex-cm-ideal-pendulum-equation-and-stability.md:82:[F5] Trigonometry and calculus: $\cos^2\theta+\sin^2\theta=1$, so $|\cos\theta|\le1$, with $\cos\theta=1$ only when $\theta\in2\pi\mathbb Z$ ([[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]], [[thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period]]); sums and products of differentiable functions are differentiable with the usual rules ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]), and a differentiable function with vanishing derivative on an interval is constant ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]], [[def-interval]], [[def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix]]).
items/thm-qftx-photon-rotations-helicity.md:100:4.1 By step 1.4, $R_{\mathbf a}'(0)=[\mathbf a]_\times$. For $f\in\mathcal D$, differentiating $G(t,\mathbf k)=R_{\mathbf a}(t)f(R_{\mathbf a}(-t)\mathbf k)$ at $t=0$ gives $\partial_tG(0,\mathbf k)=(\mathbf a\times f)(\mathbf k)-(\mathbf a\times\mathbf k)\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf k}f(\mathbf k)$, which equals $-\frac{i}{\hbar}(L_{\mathbf a}+S_{\mathbf a}^{\mathrm{op}})f$ because $-iS_{\mathbf a}v=\mathbf a\times v$. If $f\ne0$, use the closed radial annulus $K$ from step 2.2 containing its compact support; for $t$ near zero each $G(t,\cdot)$ is supported in $K$. The product of a compact time interval and $K$ is a closed bounded subset of $\mathbb R^4$, hence compact by [F4], and $G$ and its $t$-derivative are continuous there. Thus [F4] makes $\partial_tG$ uniformly continuous. For each fixed $\mathbf k\in K$ and each of the six real coordinate functions $g(s)$ of $G(s,\mathbf k)\in\mathbb C^3\cong\mathbb R^6$, set $m=(g(t)-g(0))/t$ for $t\ne0$. The function $g(s)-ms$ has equal endpoint values on the compact interval between $0$ and $t$. If it is nonconstant, [F6] gives an interior maximum or minimum. Difference quotients from the two sides then have opposite weak signs, so differentiability forces its derivative there to be zero. If it is constant, its derivative is zero everywhere. Thus $m=g'(c)$ for some $c$ between $0$ and $t$. Uniform continuity and $|c|\le|t|$ imply that the difference quotients of $G$ converge uniformly on $K$ to $\partial_tG(0,\cdot)$. Since $\nu(K)<\infty$ by [F2], this gives convergence in $L^2(d\nu)$; the quotients lie in the closed subspace $\mathcal H_T^\nu$ by step 1.1, so the limit is the norm derivative in that space. For $f=0$ the derivative is immediate. By the Stone derivative characterization in step 3.1, $f\in D(J_{\mathbf a})$ and $J_{\mathbf a}f=(L_{\mathbf a}+S_{\mathbf a}^{\mathrm{op}})f$. [F2, F4, F6, step 1.1, step 1.4, step 2.2, step 3.1, algebra]
