items/pthm-gr-flrw-cosmological-redshift.md:138:[L5] Differentiable functions of the parameter satisfy the chain and product rules ([[def-derivative]], [[thm-chain-rule]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]). A function continuous on an interval and with zero derivative at its interior points is constant on the whole interval ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]).
items/pthm-gr-flrw-cosmological-redshift.md:146:2.1 (Nullness gives a conserved product.) Nullness and [L1] give $0=-c^2(V^t)^2+a^2\gamma_{ij}V^iV^j$, so $\gamma_{ij}V^iV^j=c^2(V^t)^2/a^2$. Substitution in step 1.1 yields $\dot V^t+H(V^t)^2=0$. By the product and chain rules [L5], $\frac{d}{ds}(a(t(\eta(s)))V^t(s))=\dot a(t(\eta(s)))(V^t(s))^2+a(t(\eta(s)))\dot V^t(s)=aH(V^t)^2-aH(V^t)^2=0$. This calculation holds in a product chart near each ray point; the product is the globally defined $C^1$ function $a(t\circ\eta)\,dt(\eta')$, so its derivative vanishes everywhere in the interior of $J$. It is continuous on $J$, including any endpoints present. Since $J$ is an interval, [L5] makes this product constant on all of $J$, proving (a) and its endpoint evaluation. [L1, L5, step 1.1, algebra]
