items/pthm-em-uniform-ohmic-modal-decay.md:88:[F6] Distributional and one-variable tools: distributional derivative and its compatibility with $C^1$ regular distributions, with differentiation commuting with the embedding ([[def-distributional-derivative]], [[thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes]]); a distribution with zero derivative on a connected open set is constant ([[thm-a-distribution-with-zero-derivatives-on-a-connected-open-set-is-constant]]); the oriented primitive of a continuous function is $C^1$ with the given derivative and continuous functions on compact intervals are Riemann integrable ([[thm-ftc-first-part]], [[thm-ftc-second-part]], [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]); dominated convergence for passing integrals of uniformly convergent families ([[thm-dominated-convergence]]).
items/pthm-em-uniform-ohmic-modal-decay.md:108:2.1 [Rectangular class: global solutions, energy and convergence] For every initial pair in the rectangular class of [F2] the damped system of [F1] has a unique $C(\mathbb R;L^2)$ distributional solution in the class, finite Fourier mode sums converge in energy norm uniformly on compact time intervals and are dense at every time, and the coefficient equations of step 1.1 hold for every wavevector. Indeed, for each $k$ the right-hand side of the finite-dimensional system of step 1.1 is globally state-Lipschitz (it is affine), so [F7] gives a unique global solution through the initial coefficients; differentiate the per-mode energy $\varepsilon|\widehat E|^2+\mu^{-1}|\widehat B|^2$. With $C:=\overline{\widehat E}\cdot(k\times\widehat B)$ and $\overline{\widehat B}\cdot(k\times\widehat E)=-\overline C$, the curl contribution is $(2/\mu)\operatorname{Re}(iC+i\overline C)=0$ by the scalar triple-product identity of [F8] and complex conjugation [F9]; this gives $\frac{d}{dt}\bigl(\varepsilon|\widehat E|^2+\mu^{-1}|\widehat B|^2\bigr)=-2\sigma|\widehat E|^2\le0$, so each modal energy is nonincreasing forward and bounded by $e^{2\gamma T}$ times its initial value on $[-T,T]$ (multiply by $e^{2\gamma t}$ and differentiate); Parseval for the complete class [F2] therefore controls the energy-norm of every omitted tail uniformly on compact times. Finite solutions converge in $C_tL^2$ to a limit in the class, and the linear distributional equations pass to the limit against compactly supported smooth periodic tests by Cauchy–Schwarz [F5] on a compact time support, exactly as in the lossless argument of [F2]; the constraints $k\cdot\widehat E=k\cdot\widehat B=0$ and the parities are preserved by step 1.1, so the limit lies in the same class. Uniqueness: a $C_tL^2$ weak solution has continuous coefficients whose distributional derivatives equal the displayed continuous right-hand sides; for each coefficient equation, subtract the oriented primitive of the continuous right-hand side, which is $C^1$ with that derivative by [F6], use the $C^1$ regular-distribution compatibility of [F6] to identify its distributional derivative, conclude that the difference has zero distributional derivative, hence is constant by [F6], and match the initial value; global uniqueness of the finite system identifies the coefficients, and completeness of the Fourier class identifies the fields. [step 1.1, F2, F5, F6, F7, F8, F9]
items/pthm-em-uniform-ohmic-modal-decay.md:116:5.1 [Decay, zero sectors and long-time limits] For $\sigma>0$ (so $\gamma>0$) every positive mode decays: if $\Delta_j=\gamma^2/4-v^2\lambda_j>0$ the two real roots $-\gamma/2\pm\sqrt{\Delta_j}$ are negative; if $\Delta_j<0$ the complex roots have real part $-\gamma/2<0$, and the oscillatory factor is bounded by the cosine and sine identities of [F7]; if $\Delta_j=0$ the factor $t e^{-\gamma t/2}$ tends to zero by [F7]. The positive-mode derivatives $a_j'$ also tend to zero from the same explicit formulas, so $b_j=(a_j'+\gamma a_j)/(v^2\sqrt{\lambda_j})\to0$ for each positive mode. Electric zero modes obey $a_j(t)=a_j(0)e^{-\gamma t}$ by step 1.3, and magnetic kernel data stay constant by step 1.2, so in the smooth class the solution tends in energy norm as $t\to+\infty$ to $(0,P_{K_M}B(0))$, where $P_{K_M}$ is the orthogonal projection onto $K_M$ of step 1.2; the finite-head/finite-tail argument of step 3.1 passes the modewise convergence to the whole energy norm. For $\sigma=0$ the energy is constant by step 4.1, the electric zero modes are static by step 1.3 and the original lossless branch is recovered. The zero sectors are retained, not divided by their eigenvalues, and no universal decay to zero in the presence of magnetic kernel data is claimed. [step 1.2, step 1.3, step 3.1, step 4.1, F7]
items/lem-csm-frontier-two-velocity-contraction.md:102:1.1 For each $n\in\mathbb Z$, set $v=c/\epsilon$, $\alpha=\lambda/\epsilon^2$, $M_n=\begin{pmatrix}-i\kappa_n v-\alpha&\alpha\\ \alpha&i\kappa_n v-\alpha\end{pmatrix}$, and $U_n(0)=(\widehat f_{+,0}(n),\widehat f_{-,0}(n))^{\mathsf T}$. Realify $z\in\mathbb C^2$ by $\mathcal Rz=(\operatorname{Re}z_1,\operatorname{Re}z_2,\operatorname{Im}z_1,\operatorname{Im}z_2)\in\mathbb R^4$, and for $M=P+iQ$ put $\mathcal RM=\begin{pmatrix}P&-Q\\Q&P\end{pmatrix}$. Direct coordinate multiplication gives $\mathcal R(Mz)=(\mathcal RM)\mathcal Rz$ and $|\mathcal Rz|_2^2=|z_1|^2+|z_2|^2$. By [F6], $\mathbb R^4$ is Banach; [F7] applied to each row bounds every real $4\times4$ matrix as an operator, and [F8]–[F9] specify the Euclidean and operator norms. By [F5], $\mathcal RU_n(t):=e^{t\mathcal RM_n}\mathcal RU_n(0)$ defines a solution of the realified mode IVP for all $t\ge0$, and pulling back by $\mathcal R$ defines $U_n(t)$. The matrix-valued ODE supplier [F4] gives uniqueness of this vector solution on each compact interval $[0,T]$: embed any vector solution as the first column of a $4\times4$ matrix solution with the other columns zero, and apply [F4]. For any smooth solution of the PDE, integration by parts in real and imaginary parts separately, using [F3], gives these same Fourier mode equations, so the constructed modes are the only possible ones. Define the candidate pair by the Fourier series $F^\epsilon(t,x):=\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}U_n(t)e_n(x)$, with $e_n$ from [F1]. Since real initial data satisfy $U_{-n}(0)=\overline{U_n(0)}$ and $M_{-n}=\overline{M_n}$, uniqueness gives $U_{-n}(t)=\overline{U_n(t)}$, so this series is real. The skew-Hermitian transport part contributes zero to the derivative of $|U_n|^2$, and direct multiplication of the collision matrix gives $(|U_n|^2)'=-2\alpha|\widehat f_+^\epsilon(n,t)-\widehat f_-^\epsilon(n,t)|^2\le0$. For every integer $r\ge0$ and $q>r+1$, periodicity and [F3] give $\widehat{\partial_x^q f_{\pm,0}}(n)=(i\kappa_n)^q\widehat f_{\pm,0}(n)$ by repeated integration by parts; hence $|\widehat f_{\pm,0}(n)|\le |\kappa_n|^{-q}\int_0^L|\partial_x^qf_{\pm,0}(x)|\,dm_L(x)$ for $n\ne0$. Grouping $2^j\le |n|<2^{j+1}$ then shows $\sum_n(1+|\kappa_n|)^r|U_n(0)|<\infty$. The mode bound $|U_n(t)|\le|U_n(0)|$ and $|M_n|=O(1+|n|)$ imply absolute uniform convergence on every compact time interval of the Fourier series and of each differentiated series, since each space derivative contributes a factor $i\kappa_n$ and each time derivative a factor $M_n$. Fourier orthonormality [F2] gives the series coefficients $U_n$; their mode equations and the uniformly convergent differentiated series then verify the PDE termwise. At $t=0$ the series agrees with the prescribed smooth data by Fourier completeness [F2], first in $L^2$ and hence pointwise by continuity. Thus it is a global smooth solution of the Cauchy problem. Any other smooth solution has the same mode equations and initial coefficients; [A1] and [F2] make the solutions equal as $L^2(\mathbb T_L,dm_L;\mathbb C^2)$ classes, hence almost everywhere. Both solutions are smooth and therefore continuous in $x$. If their difference were nonzero at some $x_0$, continuity would give a nonempty open arc on which its norm is bounded below by a positive constant; every nonempty open arc has positive normalized Lebesgue measure $dm_L$, contradicting equality almost everywhere. Thus they agree pointwise on $\mathbb T_L$. [A1, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, F7, F8, F9, given, algebra]
