items/pthm-cm11-principal-rotations.md:119:[L4] Matrix products: $(Rl)'=R'l+Rl'$ for the $C^{1}$ curves $R:I_0\to SO(3)$ and $l:I_0\to\mathbb R^{3}$; $R'=R[\omega]_\times$; and a continuous curve on an interval with vanishing derivative is constant ([[lem-cm-matrix-curve-derivative-rules]], [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]).
items/lem-gr-parallel-transport-and-geodesic-speed.md:13:deps: [thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative, def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time, lem-gr-proper-time-reparametrization-and-four-velocity, def-covariant-derivative-along-a-curve, thm-covariant-derivative-along-a-curve-is-independent-of-frame-and-extension, def-geodesic-of-an-affine-connection, def-parallel-section-along-a-curve, def-parallel-transport-along-a-piecewise-smooth-curve, thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-parallel-sections, def-christoffel-symbols-of-an-affine-connection, lem-gr-connection-conditions-in-chart-components, def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects, def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors, def-gr-metric-selected-connection, thm-coordinate-derivations-form-a-basis-of-the-tangent-space, cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant, def-countable-choice]
items/lem-gr-parallel-transport-and-geodesic-speed.md:113:[L7] A differentiable real function on an interval with zero derivative is constant ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]), applied on compact subintervals and extended to included endpoints by continuity. Applied to chart components, this also makes a curve with zero velocity locally constant and hence constant on its connected interval.
items/lem-gr-parallel-transport-and-geodesic-speed.md:123:3.1 (Parallel transport.) On a singleton, [L4] and the transport definition give the unique prescribed vector and identity map, which preserves the metric. For $a<b$, let $\gamma:[a,b]\to M$ be piecewise smooth and $v\in T_{\gamma(a)}M$. The existence and uniqueness of a continuous piecewise parallel section through $v$ is [L4]; the map $P_\gamma$ is therefore well defined, and it is linear because the difference of two parallel sections with initial values $v,w$ is a parallel section (linearity of $D_t$ from step 1.1) with initial value $v-w$. It is bijective with inverse given by the same construction for the reversed curve. For $v,w$ apply claim 1 to their parallel sections $V,W$ on each smooth piece: $g(V,W)$ has zero derivative there and is continuous across corners, hence constant on $[a,b]$ by [L7], so $g(P_\gamma v,P_\gamma w)=g(v,w)$. [step 2.1, L4, L7, algebra]
items/lem-csm-frontier-finite-box-cube-max-fiber-influence.md:80:6.1 If $M=0$, steps 4.1–5.1 make every bit derivative of every $g_m$ zero, so every $g_m$ is constant, and step 2.2 gives $a(1-a)=0$. If $M>0$, apply the variance bound of step 4.2 to $g_m$, using steps 4.1–5.1. Each nonzero denominator is at least $1+\log(1/M)$, giving $\operatorname{Var}(g_m)\le2\sum_iF_i/(1+\log(1/M))$. Taking the limit with the explicit error in step 2.2 proves the first stated inequality. [step 4.2, step 2.2, step 4.1, step 5.1, algebra]
items/thm-qftx-scalar-eg-induction.md:159:5.1 For a fixed smooth real function $h$ define the field shift $(\tau_hF)(\psi)=F(\psi+h)$ and the directional variation $D_hF(\psi)=\left.\frac{d}{ds}F(\psi+sh)\right|_{s=0}$. Both preserve the local polynomial class, and $\operatorname{supp}(\tau_hF)\subseteq\operatorname{supp}F$. In addition to the contract, maintain inductively $D_hT_k(F_1,\ldots,F_k)=\sum_iT_k(F_1,\ldots,D_hF_i,\ldots,F_k)$ for every smooth $h$; compact input supports make this meaningful without compact support of $h$. It holds for $k=0,1$. The finite contraction formula makes $D_h$ a derivation of $\star_W$, since $W$ is field independent and directional variations commute with every input variation. Hence the causal block products, and then their glued distribution $T_n^0$, obey the same identity off $\Delta_n$. Applying it along $s\mapsto T_n^0(\tau_{sh}F_1,\ldots,\tau_{sh}F_n)(\psi-sh)$ gives zero derivative, so $T_n^0(\tau_hF_1,\ldots,\tau_hF_n)(\psi)=T_n^0(F_1,\ldots,F_n)(\psi+h)$. All expressions are finite polynomials in $s$, so this reasoning is algebraic. Put $\nu_n^0=T_n^0|_{\psi=0}$, meaning the numerical density-label distributions with smearings away from $\Delta_n$. They determine the full punctured product by the preceding shift identity, and satisfy the numerical total-derivative identities because evaluation at zero commutes with coordinate differentiation. During extension and Lorentz normalization make choices only for this numerical family; graphwise it consists of the fully contracted coefficients. Constant entries are fixed by the lower maps as in step 1.1. [F1, F2, F3, F11, step 1.1, step 4.1, construct, algebra]
items/prop-gr-fx-bianchi-shear-decay.md:71:[F4] If a real-valued function is continuous on an interval and differentiable at every interior point with zero derivative there, then it is constant on the interval ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]).
items/prop-gr-fx-bianchi-shear-decay.md:81:3.1 Differentiating $V=a_1a_2a_3$ gives $\dot V=V\sum_k\dot a_k/a_k=VH_\Sigma$. Multiplying the equation in step 2.1 by $V$ and applying the product rule yields $\frac{d}{dt}(V\Delta_{ij})=c^2V(Q_i-Q_j)$. Each $H_i$ is $C^1$ because $a_i$ is positive and $C^2$, so $V\Delta_{ij}$ is continuous on $I$ and differentiable at every point of $I$. When the diagonal source is isotropic its derivative vanishes; [F4] then makes $V\Delta_{ij}$ constant on $I$. For unequal sources the displayed derivative retains the stated forcing term. [step 2.1, F1, F4, algebra]
items/cex-gr-negative-sound-speed-derivative-breaks-evolution.md:75:2.1 (Failure of fixed-Sobolev continuity on smooth modes.) Fix $\tau>0$ and an integer $r\ge0$. In step 1.1 take $A_n=(1+n^2)^{-r/2}e^{-an\tau/2}$. Under the derivative-sum norm of [L3], $\|\cos(nx^1)\|_{H^r}^2=\tfrac12\sum_{j=0}^r n^{2j}$, which lies between positive constants times $(1+n^2)^r$, uniformly for $n\ge1$. Consequently the initial H^r norm tends to zero, whereas the density H^r norm at $\tau$ is at least a positive constant times $e^{an\tau/2}$ and tends to infinity. Each datum and solution is a single trigonometric polynomial and hence smooth; indeed the data tend to zero in every fixed derivative norm because exponential decay dominates every power. Any other classical solution of the same datum has the same coefficient at $(n,0,0)$ by step 1.1. For any such density lying in $H^r$, integration by parts gives $n^r|\widehat f(n,0,0)|=|\widehat{D_1^rf}(n,0,0)|\le\|D_1^rf\|_2\le\|f\|_{H^r}$. The coefficient inequality follows from $|\widehat h(k)|\le\int|h|\le\|h\|_2$; the second bound follows by expanding $0\le\int(|h|-\int|h|)^2$ on the normalized probability space. Consequently every classical solution choice has density $H^r$ norm at least a positive constant times $e^{an\tau/2}$. Thus no choice of a solution operator, even restricted to these smooth trigonometric data, can be continuous at zero from $H^r$ data to $H^r$ values at any fixed positive time. [step 1.1, L3, construct, algebra]
items/ex-gr-curvature-in-a-point-normal-chart.md:83:1.2 (Christoffel symbols.) By [L1], the only nonzero first derivative of a metric component is $\partial_0g_{11}=2x^0$, so a symbol can be nonzero only if one of its indices is a derivative index $0$ and another index refers to the component $g_{11}$. Checking the three slots: $\Gamma^1{}_{10}=\Gamma^1{}_{01}=\tfrac12g^{11}\partial_0g_{11}=\tfrac12(1+(x^0)^2)^{-1}(2x^0)=\frac{x^0}{1+(x^0)^2}$, and $\Gamma^0{}_{11}=\tfrac12g^{00}(-\partial_0g_{11})=\tfrac12(-1)(-2x^0)=x^0$; all remaining symbols have a factor $\partial_ag_{bc}$ with $a\ne0$ or $g_{bc}$ one of the constant components and vanish. [L1, algebra]
