items/lem-em-rectangular-pec-energy-completeness.md:62:[L4] **Distributional derivatives.** The distributional derivative is defined by $\langle\partial^\alpha u,\varphi\rangle=(-1)^{|\alpha|}\langle u,\partial^\alpha\varphi\rangle$ ([[def-distributional-derivative]]), and distributional differentiation commutes; a $C^k$ function is a regular distribution whose distributional derivatives are its classical ones ([[thm-distributional-differentiation-is-continuous-and-commutes]]). A distribution on the connected open set $\mathbb R$ with all first derivatives zero is a constant ([[thm-a-distribution-with-zero-derivatives-on-a-connected-open-set-is-constant]]). Moreover continuous functions on compact intervals are Riemann integrable ([[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]), their oriented integral functions are $C^1$ with the integrand as derivative ([[thm-ftc-first-part]]), and a differentiable function with integrable derivative is recovered from its derivative ([[thm-ftc-second-part]]).
items/lem-em-rectangular-pec-energy-completeness.md:76:4.2 Let $(E,B)\in C(\mathbb R;\mathcal C)$ be any weak solution in the sense of step 1.1, and fix a mode index $m$ together with a real mode function $\varphi$ in the finite-dimensional space of modes belonging to the orbit $O_{p^{(m)}}$ (a smooth periodic vector field). Testing the two weak equations with $\psi(t)\varphi(x)$, where $\psi\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R)$ is arbitrary, gives, in the sense of distributions on $\mathbb R$, the scalar equations $c_\varphi'=(\text{the corresponding finite linear combination of the other modal coefficients})$, where $c_\varphi(t)=\langle\varphi,(E,B)(t)\rangle$ is the coefficient pairing; these are continuous functions of $t$ because $(E,B)$ is continuous into $L^2$ and the pairing is continuous. Assembling the finitely many coefficients belonging to $O_{p^{(m)}}$ into a real vector $Z_m(t)$ gives a finite linear system $\partial_tZ_m=A_mZ_m$ with continuous right-hand side; applying the primitive construction componentwise on a compact interval $[a,b]$, every component of $A_mZ_m$ is continuous, hence Riemann integrable [L4], its oriented primitive $G$ is $C^1$ with $G'=g$ [L4], and the regular-distribution compatibility clause of [L4] (whose use here is covered by the standing $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$) transfers this to distributional derivatives; subtracting the primitive from $Z_m$ shows that the difference has zero distributional derivative, hence is constant on the connected set $\mathbb R$ [L4]; continuity makes the equality pointwise. Therefore every modal coefficient is a classical $C^1$ solution of its finite linear system. Since $Z_m$ solves a linear constant-coefficient ODE, its value at every time is determined by $Z_m(0)$ by the global uniqueness part of [L3]; hence the weak solution $(E,B)$ is uniquely determined by its initial datum $(E(0),B(0))$, because the modal coefficients determine the fields by the completeness in step 3.1. [step 3.1, step 1.1, L4, L3]
items/pthm-rpm-constant-electric-orbit.md:100:[F8] [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]: a differentiable scalar function on an interval with zero derivative is constant there.
items/thm-fd-capillary-gravity-dispersion-energy.md:126:[F8] [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]: a real function continuous on an order-convex interval and differentiable with derivative zero at every interior point is constant on the whole interval.
items/lem-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-cc-arbitrary-hilbert-spectral-calculus.md:91:6.3 Strong groups, precise derivative domain and group uniqueness: define $U_A(t)=b_t(A)$ with $b_t(\lambda)=e^{it\lambda}$. Step 4.1 gives unitary group laws on $H$. For every fixed $x$, this is the local strongly continuous group on $K_x$; hence it is strongly continuous on all of $H$. If $x\in D(A)$, the exact local derivative in [F1] gives $(U_A(t)x-x)/t\to iAx$. Conversely, if those quotients converge in $H$, all belong to the closed space $K_x$, so their limit belongs to $K_x$ and the local derivative criterion gives $x\in D(A_{K_x})=D(A)\cap K_x$. Domain invariance, commutation with $A$ and preservation of both $\|x\|$ and $\|Ax\|$ also follow in $K_x$, proving graph-norm invariance. If another strongly continuous unitary group $V(t)$ has this exact generator $iA$, its group law gives $V(t)D(A)=D(A)$ and $AV(t)x=V(t)Ax$ by taking the derivative at zero in $V(s)V(t)x=V(t)V(s)x$. For $x\in D(A)$, differentiate $U_A(-t)V(t)x$: the two derivatives cancel, so every scalar pairing of this vector-valued function has zero derivative and is constant by the scalar fundamental theorem. Therefore $V(t)x=U_A(t)x$ on the dense domain; boundedness extends equality to all $H$. This supplies the arbitrary-H autonomous group and exact generator without a global separability assumption or an AC Stone theorem. [F1, step 2.1, step 4.1, step 5.1, algebra]
items/pthm-tgr-reduced-horizon-temperature.md:91:[F6] [[cor-mean-value-theorem]] implies that a differentiable function with zero derivative on an interval is constant there.
items/pthm-tgr-reduced-horizon-temperature.md:103:2.1 Put $R=r_\uparrow+r_\downarrow$ and $p_*=r_\uparrow/R$. Then $0<p_*<1$, and $p(t)=p_*+(p(0)-p_*)e^{-Rt}$ satisfies the adopted equation and initial condition by [F5]. Since $R>0$, [F5] and [F7] give $0<e^{-Rt}\le1$ for $t\ge0$; hence $p(t)=e^{-Rt}p(0)+(1-e^{-Rt})p_*$ is a probability. For any other differentiable solution, $q(t)=e^{Rt}(p(t)-p_*)$ has zero derivative by the rate equation and [F5]; [F6] makes $q$ constant on every compact time interval, so the displayed solution is unique. Its stationary value is therefore $p_*$. [A1, F5, F6, F7, step 1.1, algebra]
items/pthm-td-joule-thomson-chart.md:9:physical_scope: Local derivative identity for a closed-composition system on a positive-temperature C2 Gibbs response chart with nonzero constant-pressure heat capacity; it does not assert an open-flow throttle balance, a sign of cooling, or an empirical material coefficient.
