items/lem-nrqm-potentials-one-dimensional-flux-identity.md:80:[F4] **Zero derivative implies constancy.** If $I\subseteq\mathbb R$ is order-convex and $f:I\to\mathbb R$ is continuous on $I$ and differentiable at every interior point of $I$ with $f'(x)=0$ there, then $f$ is constant on $I$ ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]], [[def-interval]]).
items/thm-nrqmx-spin-cover-projective-rotations.md:143:2.1 By [F1], the irreducibility hypothesis leaves one spin-$s$ block, with orthonormal basis $v_k$, $k=0,\ldots,2s$, and $J_zv_k=(s-k)v_k$. Write $\mu_k=s-k$ and $u_k(t)=U(t)v_k$. Step 1.1 gives $u_k'(t)=-i\mu_k u_k(t)$. From [F5] and [F6], the scalar map $t\mapsto\exp(i\mu_k t)$ has derivative $i\mu_k\exp(i\mu_k t)$: its difference quotient at $t$ factors as $\exp(i\mu_k t)(\exp(i\mu_k h)-1)/h$ and the second factor tends to $i\mu_k$. The coordinate product rule established in step 1.1 gives $\frac{d}{dt}(\exp(i\mu_k t)u_k(t))=0$. To justify constancy, write this vector in the fixed finite orthonormal basis of [F1]. Every real and imaginary coordinate has zero derivative on $\mathbb R$ and is continuous (its increment is its difference quotient times the real increment). For arbitrary $a<b$, apply [F12] separately to these real coordinate functions on $[a,b]$; each has equal endpoint values. All coordinates, and hence the vector, are therefore constant on $\mathbb R$ and equal $v_k$ at $t=0$. Consequently $U(t)v_k=\exp(-i\mu_k t)v_k$. [F1, F5, F6, F11, F12, step 1.1, algebra]
