items/pthm-cm03-energy-balance.md:83:[L4] Newton–Leibniz ([[thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative]]): if $G$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$ with integrable derivative $G'$, then $\int_{a}^{b}G'=G(b)-G(a)$; a real function on an interval with zero derivative at every interior point is constant ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]); the interval $J$ has nonempty interior ([[def-interval]], [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]).
items/pthm-em-gauss-constraint-propagation.md:163:[F3] The divergence of a curl vanishes for $C^2$ fields, and mixed partial derivatives commute ([[thm-the-divergence-of-a-curl-vanishes]], [[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]]); a differentiable function on an open interval with identically zero derivative is constant ([[cor-mean-value-theorem]], [[def-interval]]).
items/pthm-em-gauss-constraint-propagation.md:173:1.1 In the smooth branch, mixed partials commute and the divergence of a curl vanishes, so $\partial_tD_B=\operatorname{div}(\partial_tB)=-\operatorname{div}(\operatorname{curl}E)=0$ pointwise, and $\partial_tD_E=\operatorname{div}(\partial_tE)-\varepsilon_0^{-1}\partial_t\rho=c^2\operatorname{div}(\operatorname{curl}B)-c^2\mu_0\operatorname{div}J-\varepsilon_0^{-1}\partial_t\rho=-\varepsilon_0^{-1}(\operatorname{div}J+\partial_t\rho)=0$ pointwise, using $c^2\mu_0=\varepsilon_0^{-1}$ and the separately assumed continuity in the last equality. For each fixed $x$ the two functions $t\mapsto D_B(x,t)$ and $t\mapsto D_E(x,t)$ are differentiable on the interval $I$ with zero derivative, hence constant; therefore zero constraints at any $t_0$ persist at every time and a nonzero value at a point persists unchanged. [F1, F3, given, algebra]
items/pthm-em-gauss-constraint-propagation.md:177:1.3 For the continuous-representative statement assume $\partial_th=0$ in $\mathcal D'(U)$ for a continuous $h$, and let $\varphi\in C_c^\infty(\Omega)$. The function $F(t):=\int_\Omega h(x,t)\varphi(x)\,d\lambda_3(x)$ is continuous on $I$: on a compact time interval the integrands have the fixed compact spatial support of $\varphi$ and a uniform bound coming from continuity of $h$ on the compact set $\operatorname{supp}\varphi\times$ that interval, so dominated convergence applies. For $\eta\in C_c^\infty(I)$ with $\int_I\eta=0$, extend $\eta$ by zero and put $H(t):=\int_{-\infty}^t\eta(v)\,dv$; the FTC makes $H$ smooth with $H'=\eta$, and, choosing $a<b$ in $I$ with $\operatorname{supp}\eta\subset(a,b)$, $H$ vanishes outside $[a,b]$: it is zero before $a$ and equals the zero total integral after $b$. Thus $\operatorname{supp}H\subseteq[a,b]\subset I$, so $\Psi(x,t):=\varphi(x)H(t)$ is a compact smooth test with $\partial_t\Psi=\varphi\eta$; applying the derivative equation to this primitive test gives $0=\langle\partial_th,\Psi\rangle=-\langle h,\partial_t\Psi\rangle=-\int_IF(t)\eta(t)\,dt$, the multiple integral being identified with the iterated one by the Borel product structure and Fubini and its Riemann-Lebesgue value by the comparison lemma. Choosing a nonnegative compact bump $\beta$ of integral one and writing an arbitrary temporal test as $\eta=\eta_0+(\int_I\eta)\beta$ with $\int_I\eta_0=0$ gives $\int_I(F-C)\eta_0=0$ for all $\eta_0$ with zero integral and $C:=\int_IF\beta$; since also $\int_I(F-C)\beta=\int_IF\beta-C\int_I\beta=0$, every $\eta\in C_c^\infty(I)$ satisfies $\int_I(F-C)\eta=0$, and the sign-and-bump argument (a nonzero value of the continuous $F-C$ at a point persists with one sign on a subinterval of positive measure, and a nonnegative bump equal to one on a smaller subinterval has strictly nonzero pairing by monotonicity and the positive finite measure of that subinterval) then forces $F-C=0$, that is, $F=C$ constant on $I$. For complex-valued functions this sign-and-bump argument is applied separately to their real and imaginary parts with real tests; it also applies componentwise to the spatial difference below. Since $\varphi$ is arbitrary, $h(\cdot,t_1)-h(\cdot,t_2)$ is a continuous function on $\Omega$ whose pairing against every spatial test vanishes, so the same sign-and-bump argument in $\mathbb R^3$ gives $h(\cdot,t_1)=h(\cdot,t_2)$; hence $h$ is independent of $t$. [F4, F6, given, algebra]
items/pthm-fd-bgk-equilibration.md:162:[F8] [[cor-mean-value-theorem]]: a differentiable function with zero derivative on an interval is constant there, by applying the theorem to any compact subinterval.
items/lem-gr-proper-time-reparametrization-and-four-velocity.md:121:[L7] A continuous nowhere-zero derivative has constant sign by the intermediate value theorem ([[thm-intermediate-value]]); the mean value theorem gives strict monotonicity for a derivative of fixed strict sign ([[cor-mean-value-theorem]]). The $C^1$ chain rule holds in coordinates ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]); a $C^1$ function of one real variable with nonzero derivative is a local $C^1$ diffeomorphism at every point ([[thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem]]).
items/ex-cm04-damped-regimes-check.md:115:3.1 Energy. With $m=k=1$ the energy is $E=\tfrac12v^{2}+\tfrac12x^{2}$, and $E(0)=\tfrac12\cdot0+\tfrac12\cdot1=\tfrac12$ J in all three cases. By [L1] the identity $E'(t)=-c\,x'(t)^{2}$ holds along each motion, so $E'(t)<0$ exactly when $x'(t)\ne0$. In the first case the exceptional times are $t=2\pi n/\sqrt3$ for every $n\in\mathbb Z$, by the sine zero set and the positive exponential factor in step 2.1. In the second and third cases the derivative vanishes only at $t=0$ and $E'(t)<0$ for every $t\ne0$. In particular no motion here is a constant-energy motion other than the rest motion, consistent with clause 2 of [[pthm-cm04-damped-motion]]. [step 1.1, step 2.1, L1, L2, algebra]
items/pthm-td-regular-gibbs-phase-rule.md:85:[F2] [[post-td-entropy-maximum]] and [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]: The adopted complete-compound entropy is the sum of the separated phase entropies and the explicitly supplied auxiliary entropies, and the stable endpoint is a global maximum on its exact feasible set. Along each stipulated pairwise transfer curve the auxiliary states are fixed. Along each reaction curve the total auxiliary entropy is assumed differentiable at zero extent with derivative zero; individual auxiliary states and entropy maps need not be constant or individually differentiable.
items/pthm-nrqm-kinematics-boost-covariance.md:188:[F6] A real function continuous on a closed interval and differentiable in its interior with zero derivative is constant, by [[cor-mean-value-theorem]]; restrictions of a differentiable curve to compact intervals satisfy these continuity hypotheses.
items/thm-qft-bounded-regulator-dyson-factorization.md:127:4.2 For a continuous vector-valued integrand, the average over a signed interval of length $h$ converges in norm to its value at the base point: the error is bounded by the supremum of its variation on that interval. Taking difference quotients in the integral equation therefore gives $\partial_tU_z(t,s)\psi=-izV(t)U_z(t,s)\psi$, with relative one-sided derivatives at the endpoints. If $u(t)=U_z(t,s)\psi$, then $$ \frac{\|u(t+h)\|^2-\|u(t)\|^2}{h} =2\operatorname{Re}\left\langle u(t),\frac{u(t+h)-u(t)}h\right\rangle +\frac{\|u(t+h)-u(t)\|^2}{h}. $$ The second term tends to zero and the first tends to $2\operatorname{Re}\langle u(t),-izV(t)u(t)\rangle=0$ for real $z$, because self-adjointness makes $\langle u,V(t)u\rangle$ real. By the continuity estimate in step 1.1, the scalar function $t\mapsto\|u(t)\|^2$ has continuous derivative zero. The real fundamental theorem [F5] on each subinterval therefore makes $\|u(t)\|^2$ constant, so $U_z(t,s)$ is an isometry. [A1, F1, F2, A2, F5, step 1.1, step 3.1, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-coulomb-kummer-recurrence-and-termination.md:93:1.1 [Substitution without dividing by a solution] Put $h(r)=r^{l+1}e^{-kr}$ and $u=h\varphi$. The multiplier $h$ is smooth and nonzero for every $r>0$, so $u\leftrightarrow\varphi$ preserves $C^2$. Its derivatives satisfy $h'/h=(l+1)/r-k$ and $h''/h=l(l+1)/r^2-2k(l+1)/r+k^2$. The product rule gives the identity $-u''+[l(l+1)/r^2-\gamma/r+k^2]u=-h[\varphi''+(2(l+1)/r-2k)\varphi'+(\gamma-2k(l+1))\varphi/r]$. Therefore the radial equation is equivalent to $r\varphi''+(2l+2-2kr)\varphi'+(\gamma-2k(l+1))\varphi=0$. Write $\varphi(r)=\widetilde v(2kr)$: then $\varphi'=2k\widetilde v'$ and $\varphi''=(2k)^2\widetilde v''$, and division by the nonzero scalar $2k$ gives $x\widetilde v''+(2l+2-x)\widetilde v'+j\widetilde v=0$. The Statement's $v(x)=e^{x/2}x^{-(l+1)}u(x/(2k))$ equals $(2k)^{-(l+1)}\widetilde v(x)$; multiplication by this constant preserves the homogeneous equation. All transformations are reversible, including at zeros of $u$, and establish (a). [F3, given, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-magnetic-and-detection-gauge-transported-propagator.md:89:[F4] A real function continuous on a closed interval and differentiable in its interior with zero derivative is constant ([[cor-mean-value-theorem]]). For an $L^2$-differentiable curve $z$, expansion of the inner product gives $(\|z\|^2)'=2\operatorname{Re}\langle z',z\rangle$. If $T$ is self-adjoint and $z\in D(T)$, then $\langle Tz,z\rangle$ is real, so $2\operatorname{Re}\langle-iTz/\hbar,z\rangle=0$.
items/pthm-rpm-static-clock-redshift.md:90:[F6] The mean value theorem applies to continuous functions on closed intervals that are differentiable on their interiors; in particular, a function differentiable on an open interval with derivative zero is constant there ([[cor-mean-value-theorem]]).
