items/ex-emg-ml-b08.md:63:[F4] [[cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound]], clauses 1–2: for a finite real vector curve on $[a,b]$, $a<b$, differentiability with integrable derivative gives $\int_a^b f'=f(b)-f(a)$; a continuous curve with derivative norm at most $M$ is $M$-Lipschitz. In particular a curve with zero derivative is constant. We apply the integral identity to smooth curves, including their one-sided endpoint derivatives; matrices are identified with their finite lists of real entries.
items/ex-emg-ml-b08.md:73:1.1 Put $B_z=\sqrt\epsilon c b_0/(\alpha L)$ and supply the real one-form $A=(B_z/2)(x^1\,dx^2-x^2\,dx^1)$ on all of Minkowski space. Its coefficients are smooth and have SI units tesla metres, and direct differentiation gives $dA=B_z\,dx^1\wedge dx^2=F$, with $(dA)_{0i}=0$ and $(dA)_{12}=B_z$. Thus the local-potential hypothesis of [F6] is met on every finite curve segment, and the action model gives its proper-time Lorentz law throughout the Example's displayed range, independently of the general small-cutoff comparison in [F1]. In flat coordinates this law has $U^0=\gamma c$, $U^i=\gamma w^i$, and $\nabla=\partial$. With $E=0$ and $F_{ij}=\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, its spatial components reduce to $d(\gamma m\mathbf w)/dt=q\mathbf w\times\mathbf B$. Its dot product with $\mathbf w$ is zero, so $|\mathbf w|$ and $\gamma=(1-|\mathbf w|^2/c^2)^{-1/2}=(1-\epsilon|\mathbf W_0|^2)^{-1/2}$ are constant. Since $\mathbf W_0$ is transverse to $\mathbf e_z$, the motion stays in that transverse plane. Define $J\mathbf v:=\mathbf v\times b_0\mathbf e_z$ there; $J$ is skew-symmetric and $J^2=-b_0^2I$. Identify the transverse plane with the real Euclidean normed space $\mathbb R^2$ using the ordered coordinate basis $(\mathbf e_x,\mathbf e_y)$; it is Banach by [F3], and $|Jv|=|b_0||v|$ proves that $J$ is bounded with $\|J\|_{2\to2}=|b_0|$. Define $E(t):=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}t^nJ^n/n!$ by [F3], and write $e^{tJ}:=E(t)$. Rescaling the exact force equation gives $\mathbf W'=J\mathbf W/\gamma$ and $\mathbf X'=\mathbf W$. The formula $\mathbf W(S)=E(S/\gamma)\mathbf W_0$ has the matched initial velocity and satisfies the velocity equation by the derivative law in [F3]; the Newton–Lorentz comparison is $\mathbf W_N(S)=E(S)\mathbf W_0$. For either constant generator $aJ$ with $a=\gamma^{-1}$ or $a=1$, any solution with this initial value is unique: differentiating $E(-aS)\mathbf W(S)$ gives zero, so [F4] makes it constant and multiplication by $E(aS)$ recovers the displayed formula. [given, F1, F3, F4, F6, A1, algebra]
