items/pthm-cm04-damped-motion.md:32:physical_scope: "The motion classification of the damped oscillator model with m>0, k>0, c>=0: the three solution families determined by the sign of the discriminant c^2-4mk (distinct real roots, double root, oscillatory roots), uniqueness and global existence for every datum, the identity E'=-c x-dot^2 for the mechanical energy E=m x-dot^2/2+k x^2/2, nonincrease of the energy, strict negativity of its derivative when c>0 and x-dot is nonzero, and the characterisation of the constant-energy motions as the rest motion when c>0. The result is a conditional deduction from the declared model data; it asserts no value of the parameters, no measured decay and no behaviour of any real damper."
items/pthm-cm04-damped-motion.md:98:[L5] [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]: a continuous real function on an interval whose derivative vanishes at every interior point is constant. [[def-interval]]: intervals are order-convex and have the stated interior points.
items/lem-nrqmx-c2-projector-chern-integrality.md:136:3.1 The contour formula for $R_\epsilon$ and its first two coordinate derivatives show $R_\epsilon\to P$ in $C^2$: differentiating $(zI-Q_\epsilon)^{-1}$ once gives $(zI-Q_\epsilon)^{-1}(\partial_jQ_\epsilon)(zI-Q_\epsilon)^{-1}$, and differentiating twice gives a finite sum of products of uniformly bounded resolvents with first and second derivatives of $Q_\epsilon$. Thus in each chart of the fixed finite partition, $R_\epsilon$ and its first derivatives converge uniformly to $P$ and its first derivatives. On the compact supports of that partition, let $\delta_0$ and $\delta_1$ be the largest of the zeroth- and first-coordinate-derivative errors, and choose $M\ge1$ bounding all first derivatives of $P$ and $R_\epsilon$ for sufficiently small $\epsilon$. For the coordinate coefficient $F_{12}(P)=P(\partial_1P\,\partial_2P-\partial_2P\,\partial_1P)P$, submultiplicativity and $\|P\|=\|R_\epsilon\|=1$ give $\|F_{12}(R_\epsilon)-F_{12}(P)\|\le4M^2\delta_0+4M\delta_1$; the trace difference is at most $d$ times this bound. Hence the continuous coefficients in the fixed finite chart-partition formula [F11] converge uniformly. Put each compact chart support in the interior of a closed nondegenerate rectangle $Q_j$. By [F12], both real and imaginary parts of each continuous zero-extended coefficient and its difference are Riemann integrable there. For a real continuous $h$ on $Q_j$, put $c=\sup_{Q_j}|h|$. Every Darboux sum of the constant functions $\pm c$ is $\pm c\operatorname{vol}(Q_j)$, since the cell volumes sum to the rectangle volume. Monotonicity in [F9] applied to $-c\le h\le c$ therefore gives $|\int_{Q_j}h|\le c\operatorname{vol}(Q_j)$. Applying this separately to the real and imaginary parts of a complex coefficient $g$ and using $|\operatorname{Re}g|,|\operatorname{Im}g|\le|g|$ gives $|\int_{Q_j}g|\le2\operatorname{vol}(Q_j)\sup_{Q_j}|g|$. Finite linearity then gives $\left|\int_\Sigma\operatorname{tr}F_{R_\epsilon}-\int_\Sigma\operatorname{tr}F_P\right|\leq2\sum_j\operatorname{vol}(Q_j)\sup_{Q_j}|\widetilde f_j^\epsilon-\widetilde f_j|\longrightarrow0$. The continuous-integral convention [F11] and this estimate show that the limit is the integral in the Statement. Step 2.1 gives the same integer for every sufficiently small $\epsilon$, so taking the limit proves the stated equality for $P$. [F2, F9, F11, F12, step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
