items/pthm-em-gauge-reachability.md:99:[F9] Calculus and topology toolkit: mixed partial derivatives of $C^2$ functions commute ([[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]]); the divergence and curl are linear and computed from first partial derivatives ([[lem-divergence-and-curl-are-linear-and-obey-the-scalar-product-rules]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]); the chain rule, the sum and product rules for partial derivatives and the componentwise $C^k$ class hold ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]); a continuous function on an interval with vanishing derivative at every interior point is constant, with the second form for functions with a common derivative ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]); the oriented integral $x\mapsto\int_{t_*}^x h$ of a continuous $h$ on an interval with at least two elements is a primitive of $h$ ([[cor-primitives-of-a-continuous-function]], [[def-oriented-integral]]); star-shapedness is the radial-segment condition ([[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]); and the intervals and the bounded graph-domain charts are those of [[def-interval]] and [[def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts]].
items/pthm-em-gauge-reachability.md:125:4.1 Branch (c): the auxiliary scalar is position independent. Fix $t\in I$. The function $x\mapsto g(x,t):=\delta\varphi(x,t)+\partial_t\chi_0(x,t)$ is $C^1$ on $\Omega$ with $\nabla g(\cdot,t)=0$ by step 3.2, and $\Omega$ is star-shaped with centre $a$, so for $x\in\Omega$ the radial path $s\mapsto g(a+s(x-a),t)$ is defined on $[0,1]$ and, by the chain rule [F9], has derivative $\langle\nabla g(a+s(x-a),t),x-a\rangle=0$ at every interior point; the zero-derivative corollary [F9] applied on $[0,1]$ makes it constant, so $g(x,t)=g(a,t)$ for every $x\in\Omega$. In particular the common value depends on $t$ only. [step 3.2, F9]
items/thm-nrqm-coulomb-atomic-dipole-matrix-element-domain.md:99:4.1 [All necessary angular branches] By [F3], $Q_qh_{lm}=R_q+r^2G_q$ has harmonic degrees $l+1,l-1$, with $G_q=(\sum c_{q,i}\partial_i h_{lm})/(2l+1)$. At $l=1$ its derivative term can be a nonzero constant in $V_0$, so the downward branch $l'=0$ is retained. At $l=0$ the derivatives vanish and only the upward branch remains. Degree orthogonality and the $L_z$ eigenvalue calculation give exactly $l'=l+1,m'=m+q$, or $l\ge1,l'=l-1,m'=m+q$. These are necessary only; the radial factor is $\int_0^\infty r\,u_{n'l'}\overline{u_{nl}}dr$ and may vanish for an allowed angular pair. No transition probability is defined. This proves (c). [step 3.1, F3, algebra]
