items/thm-sr-future-cone-and-flat-domain-of-dependence.md:133:[L10] Vector classification: $v$ is timelike, null or spacelike according as $\eta(v,v)$ is negative, zero with $v\ne0$, or positive, and the zero vector is none of these; a function with zero derivative on an interval is constant on it ([[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]], [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]], [[def-derivative]]).
items/thm-gr-smooth-cauchy-temporal-splitting.md:118:[L8] Escape lemma: a maximal integral curve with a finite endpoint leaves every compact subset of $M$ near that endpoint ([[lem-gr-maximal-flow-escape]], which assumes $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ and $\mathrm{DC}$). Calculus inputs: a differentiable function on an interval with identically vanishing derivative is constant, the chain rule holds, and a continuous function on a connected interval taking values tending to $-\infty$ and $+\infty$ at the ends attains every real value ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[cor-intermediate-value-theorem-topological]], [[def-continuity-real]]).
items/pthm-em-dipole-scattering-cross-section.md:105:[F7] Window construction toolbox: smooth compactly supported cutoffs and localized bumps exist with the properties used below ([[lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization]], [[def-mollifier-family-generated-by-a-unit-mass-smooth-bump]]); integration by parts holds on a bounding rectangle ([[thm-integration-by-parts]], [[thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles]]), and the Riemann-Lebesgue comparison identifies the Lebesgue and Riemann integrals of the continuous integrands used there ([[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]]); the first fundamental theorem gives the derivative of an integral over a moving endpoint ([[thm-ftc-first-part]]), and a continuous function on an interval with zero derivative is constant ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]). Rectangles, cubes and their volumes are those of [[def-multidimensional-rectangle-and-volume]]; continuous real functions on nondegenerate closed rectangles are Riemann integrable ([[thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable]]). The integral is monotone ([[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]]), so a nonnegative continuous cutoff equal to $1$ near a nonempty cube has a strictly positive finite integral; dividing by that integral normalizes the spatial bump.
items/ex-gr-fx02-2.md:88:2.1 Differentiating the scalar expression at $\phi=d$ with respect to a smooth direction $v$ gives $\mathcal A_0v=-8\widetilde\Delta v$. Take the nonzero constant function $v=1$. Its coordinate derivatives vanish, so $\widetilde\Delta v=0$ and $\int v\mathcal A_0v\,d\mu=0$. By [F3], the normalized measure has total mass one and the derivative Sobolev norm includes the $L^2$ norm, so $\|1\|_{H^1}^2=\int1\,d\mu=1>0$. No positive $c_0$ can satisfy the stated coercivity bound. Equivalently, the Fourier multiplier at $k=0$ is zero; [F4] identifies this constant as the zero mode. [A1, F3, F4, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
items/pthm-cm07-boundary-terms-and-gauge.md:27:physical_scope: "Gauge properties of the mechanical action of a declared potential presentation: adding to the Lagrangian the total time derivative of a C^3 function defined on the chart time-configuration domain, with the function carrying action units, changes the action by endpoint values only and leaves the Euler-Lagrange expression, the stationary curves and the model's stationary-action equation unchanged; changing the potential representative by a sufficiently regular function of time alone is an instance and shifts the action by a constant-free endpoint term; multiplying the Lagrangian by a nonzero dimensionless constant leaves the stationary curves unchanged. The statements are conditional on the adopted force branch and presentation and assert no minimisation and no empirical content."
items/pthm-cm11-euler-equations.md:171:[L6] Pairing derivative and constancy: for differentiable curves $u,v$ the scalar product $t\mapsto\langle u(t),v(t)\rangle$ is differentiable with derivative $\langle u',v\rangle+\langle u,v'\rangle$ ([[lem-cm-derivative-of-pairings-of-curves]] clause 2); a continuous real function on an interval whose derivative vanishes at every interior point is constant ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]).
items/pthm-cm11-euler-equations.md:191:3.2 Differentiating $K_{\mathrm{rot}}=\tfrac12\langle\omega,I\omega\rangle$ with [L6] and using the symmetry of $I$ from [L1] gives $K_{\mathrm{rot}}'=\langle\omega',I\omega\rangle=\langle\omega,I\omega'\rangle$; substituting $I\omega'=R^{\mathsf T}\tau-\omega\times I\omega$ from step 2.1 and using $\langle\omega,\omega\times I\omega\rangle=0$ by [L4] gives $K_{\mathrm{rot}}'=\langle\omega,R^{\mathsf T}\tau\rangle$, which is clause 3; when $\tau\equiv0$ this derivative vanishes identically and [L6] makes $K_{\mathrm{rot}}$ constant on $I_0$. The identification with the kinetic splitting is clause 3 of [[pthm-cm10-kinetic-splitting]]. [step 2.1, given, L1, L4, L6, algebra]
