items/def-flat-covariant-derivative-on-affine-minkowski-space.md:70:- **Well-definedness of (d) and (e).** In (d) the canonical isomorphism $T_pA\to V$ is independent of $p$ ([[lem-tangent-space-of-affine-minkowski-space-is-v]]), so a field along a curve is simply a $V$-valued map and its covariant derivative is its ordinary derivative; parallel transport is the identity because the equation $W'=0$ has exactly the constant solutions on an interval: each real component of $W$ is constant by [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]], and a constant vector-valued map has derivative zero. In (e) a $C^1$ field $Y$ has a continuous differential, and the derivative of $t\mapsto Y(p+tX(p))$ at $0$ is $dY_p[X(p)]$, which is a continuous function of $p$ when $Y$ and $X$ are $C^1$; only first derivatives occur in the Leibniz, metric-compatibility and torsion assertions, and the second covariant derivatives in the curvature identity require the stated $C^2$ hypotheses. No choice principle is used anywhere; all objects are finite-dimensional or explicitly parametrized.
