items/pthm-cm08-moving-chart-energy-function.md:112:[F5] Calculus and linear algebra ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-transpose-of-a-matrix]], [[def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix]], [[def-linear-map]], [[def-invertible-euclidean-linear-map]], [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]], [[def-interval]], [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]], [[def-cm01-units]]): the Euclidean inner product is symmetric and bilinear; for a linear map $A$ one has $\langle A u,w\rangle=\langle u,A^{\mathsf T}w\rangle$; the composite $x=X\circ q$ is $C^{2}$ with the displayed derivative; and $\langle X_{q}v,\mathsf M X_{q}v\rangle>0$ for $v\ne0$ because $X_{q}$ is injective and $\mathsf M$ is positive definite.
items/pthm-fd-capillary-wave-model-prediction.md:143:[L7] The real chain rule ([[thm-chain-rule]]) and product rule ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]) differentiate real-variable compositions and products. The mean value theorem ([[cor-mean-value-theorem]]) states that for real $a<b$ and $f$ continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$, there is $c\in(a,b)$ such that $f(b)-f(a)=f'(c)(b-a)$; applied to real and imaginary parts, it implies that a $C^1$ complex-valued function with zero derivative on an interval is constant.
items/thm-fd-collision-maxwellian.md:69:[F4] [[thm-ftc-second-part]]: A continuously differentiable function with zero derivative along a coordinate segment is constant along that segment.
items/pthm-em-covariant-stress-energy.md:75:Assume the smooth branch of [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]] on the laboratory region $U\cong I\times\Omega$ of [[def-em-lab-geometry]]: $E,B$ jointly $C^1$ fields of [[def-em-fields]] with the additional derivatives required by each Maxwell equation existing and continuous, continuous sources $(\rho,J)$ of [[def-em-sources]] and the constants of [[def-em-si-conventions]]; let $F$ be the electromagnetic two-form of [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]] with its fixed components, $j=(c\rho,J)$ the four-current, $u$ and $S$ the energy density and Poynting vector of [[def-em-vacuum-energy-flux]], and $g$, $\sigma$, $\Pi=-\sigma$ the momentum density, Maxwell stress and momentum flux of [[def-em-vacuum-stress-momentum]]. Define the stress-energy tensor of the field by $$T^{\mu\nu}:=\frac{1}{\mu_0}\Bigl(F^{\mu\alpha}F^\nu{}_\alpha-\frac14\eta^{\mu\nu}F^{\alpha\beta}F_{\alpha\beta}\Bigr).$$ Then: (a) [Object] $T$ is a symmetric type-$(2,0)$ tensor field of [[def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space]] with $\partial_\mu T^{\mu\nu}$ continuous on $U$, it is traceless, $\eta_{\mu\nu}T^{\mu\nu}=0$, and under a proper orthochronous change of inertial chart with transition $x'=\Lambda x+b$ its components transform as $T'^{\mu\nu}=\Lambda^\mu{}_\alpha\Lambda^\nu{}_\beta T^{\alpha\beta}$ ([[pthm-em-lorentz-field-transform]], [[thm-sr-tensor-and-current-transformation]] (iii)). (b) [Components] in the laboratory frame $$T^{00}=u=\frac{\varepsilon_0|E|^2}{2}+\frac{|B|^2}{2\mu_0},\qquad T^{0i}=T^{i0}=\frac{S_i}{c}=c\,g_i=\frac{(E\times B)_i}{\mu_0c},\qquad T^{ij}=-\sigma_{ij},$$ where $\sigma_{ij}=\varepsilon_0\bigl(E_iE_j-\tfrac12\delta_{ij}|E|^2\bigr)+\mu_0^{-1}\bigl(B_iB_j-\tfrac12\delta_{ij}|B|^2\bigr)$; in particular the spatial block is minus the Maxwell stress of [[def-em-vacuum-stress-momentum]]. (c) [Balance] under the smooth branch, $$\partial_\mu T^{\mu\nu}=-f^\nu,\qquad f^\nu:=F^{\nu\lambda}j_\lambda,$$ where algebraically $f=(J\cdot E/c,\rho E+J\times B)$. Its interpretation as the electromagnetic four-force density delivered to matter uses the separately adopted continuum coupling [[post-em-lorentz-coupling]] (ii) for matter models and sources satisfying that postulate's hypotheses. The component identities are $\partial_tu+\operatorname{div}S=-J\cdot E$ and $\partial_tg-\operatorname{div}\sigma=-(\rho E+J\times B)$ of [[pthm-em-poynting-balance]] (a) and [[pthm-em-momentum-balance]] (a). (d) [Matter coupling and finite-flux conservation] let $T_{\mathrm m}$ be a $C^1$ symmetric type-$(2,0)$ field on $U$ satisfying $\partial_\mu T_{\mathrm m}{}^{\mu\nu}=f^\nu$ (an adopted matter model, not derived here); then $\partial_\mu(T+T_{\mathrm m})^{\mu\nu}=0$, on every finite axis-parallel box with an open neighbourhood contained in $U$, the box and slice balance identities of [[thm-sr-stress-balance-and-finite-flux]] hold for the total tensor with zero divergence, retaining all side-flux terms. For each component $b$ and $c_0\le s_1<s_2\le d_0$, set $\mathcal F^b(s_1,s_2):=\sum_{i=1}^{3}\left(\int_{[s_1,s_2]\times K_i^+}T_{\mathrm{total}}{}^{ib}\,d^3x-\int_{[s_1,s_2]\times K_i^-}T_{\mathrm{total}}{}^{ib}\,d^3x\right)$, with $K:=\prod_{i=1}^{3}[c_i,d_i]$, its faces $K_i^\pm$, and $Q^b(s):=\int_K T_{\mathrm{total}}{}^{0b}(s,x)\,d^3x$ as in that theorem. Then $Q^b(s_2)-Q^b(s_1)=-\mathcal F^b(s_1,s_2)$, so $Q^b(s_2)=Q^b(s_1)$ if and only if $\mathcal F^b(s_1,s_2)=0$. In particular, $Q^b$ is constant throughout $[c_0,d_0]$ if and only if this net signed side flux vanishes on every subinterval $[s_1,s_2]$. The theorem's facewise-zero side-flux hypothesis is sufficient, but is not necessary, for constancy; zero divergence alone does not guarantee it. The angular current $M^{\lambda\mu\nu}:=x^\mu T_{\mathrm{total}}{}^{\lambda\nu}-x^\nu T_{\mathrm{total}}{}^{\lambda\mu}$ satisfies $\partial_\lambda M^{\lambda\mu\nu}=0$ by symmetry. (e) [Scope] the divergence is a product identity of $C^1$ objects and is not asserted for the weak branch, at singular source support or for any product of distributions; symmetry, zero trace and the component display are algebraic; no matter tensor is derived from vacuum Maxwell, no radiation reaction or self-force is included, and no empirical claim is made.
items/thm-cm-heavy-top-reduction.md:81:1.2 Define $E(t):=\varphi'\partial_{\varphi'}L+\theta'\partial_{\theta'}L+\psi'\partial_{\psi'}L-L$ along the trajectory. By [L4] the derivative of $t\mapsto L(q(t),q'(t))$ is $\sum_i\partial_{q_i}L\,q_i'+\sum_i\partial_{q_i'}L\,q_i''$, and substituting the three system equations $\frac{d}{dt}\partial_{q_i'}L=\partial_{q_i}L$ of [L1] turns this into $\frac{d}{dt}\bigl(\sum_iq_i'\partial_{q_i'}L\bigr)$; hence $E'=0$. Since $E$ is continuous on the interval $I$ and differentiable at its interior points, [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]] makes it constant on $I$. [given, L1, L3, L4, algebra]
