items/thm-nrqm-magnetic-and-detection-galilean-covariance.md:117:[F6] A real function continuous on a closed interval and differentiable in its interior with zero derivative is constant, by [[cor-mean-value-theorem]]; restrictions of a differentiable curve to compact intervals satisfy these continuity hypotheses.
items/thm-nrqm-magnetic-and-detection-galilean-covariance.md:131:3.1 [Uniqueness with the transformed initial datum] Let $w$ be another solution in the stated class, and put $\rho(t):=B_u(t)^{-1}w(t)$. The continuity argument of step 2.1 applies also to the inverse boost. For its $L^2$ derivative, decompose the difference quotient just as in step 2.1, using the inverse derivative from step 1.2; this gives $\rho'=i\lambda\rho+u\cdot\nabla\rho+B_u(t)^{-1}w'$. The inverse transported identities of steps 1.3-1.4 give $B_u(t)^{-1}(-\hbar^2\Delta/(2m)+M_{V_u(\cdot,t)})B_u(t)\rho=K\rho-i\hbar u\cdot\nabla\rho+m|u|^2\rho/2$. Substituting the equation for $w$ cancels the scalar and first-order terms, so $i\hbar\rho'=K\rho$ and $\rho(s)=B_u(s)^{-1}u_0$. Set $G(t):=e^{i(t-s)K/\hbar}$ and $F(t):=G(t)\rho(t)$. The quotient decomposition $G(t+h)(\rho(t+h)-\rho(t))/h+(G(t+h)-G(t))\rho(t)/h$ converges in $L^2$: the first term by strong continuity and unitarity, the second by [F1] on the fixed vector $\rho(t)\in D(K)$. Hence $F'=G(t)(\rho'+iK\rho/\hbar)=0$. A differentiable Hilbert-space curve with zero derivative is constant, by applying the real scalar mean-value theorem to every bounded real linear functional. Thus $F(t)=F(s)=B_u(s)^{-1}u_0$, and $\rho(t)=e^{-i(t-s)K/\hbar}B_u(s)^{-1}u_0$. Multiplying by $B_u(t)$ proves $w(t)=B_u(t)e^{-i(t-s)K/\hbar}B_u(s)^{-1}u_0=v(t)$. This proves the uniqueness part of (b) with its actual initial datum. [given, step 1.2, step 1.3, step 1.4, step 2.1, F1, F5, algebra, F6]
