items/thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-no-positive-compact-potential-eigenvalues.md:98:2.1 (The two reduced exterior solutions.) Define spherical Bessel functions for $z>0$ by $j_0(z)=\sin z/z$, $y_0(z)=-\cos z/z$ and the recurrences $j_{l+1}(z)=l\,j_l(z)/z-j_l'(z)$, $y_{l+1}(z)=l\,y_l(z)/z-y_l'(z)$. Put $J_l(z)=zj_l(z)$ and $Q_l(z)=zy_l(z)$, so either sequence obeys $u_{l+1}=(l+1)u_l/z-u_l'$. If $u_l''+(1-l(l+1)/z^2)u_l=0$, differentiating this recurrence twice shows $u_{l+1}''+(1-(l+1)(l+2)/z^2)u_{l+1}=0$; the initial functions $\sin z$ and $-\cos z$ solve the $l=0$ equation. Induction also shows each $J_l,Q_l$ is a finite sum of sine and cosine times polynomials in $1/z$. The recurrence therefore gives $J_l(z)=\sin(z-l\pi/2)+O(z^{-1})$, $Q_l(z)=-\cos(z-l\pi/2)+O(z^{-1})$, and the differentiated expansions $J_l'(z)=\cos(z-l\pi/2)+O(z^{-1})$, $Q_l'(z)=\sin(z-l\pi/2)+O(z^{-1})$. Their Wronskian $J_lQ_l'-J_l'Q_l$ has derivative zero by the reduced equation and limit $1$ by these expansions, so it is identically $1$. Hence $rj_l(kr)=J_l(kr)/k$ and $ry_l(kr)=Q_l(kr)/k$ are independent solutions of the radial reduced equation with $r$-Wronskian $1/k$. Every solution in step 1.2 is therefore $\alpha_{lm}(r)=A_{lm}\,rj_l(kr)+B_{lm}\,ry_l(kr)$ for fixed complex constants $A_{lm},B_{lm}$. Indeed, choose the constants matching the value and derivative at one $r_0>R$ using this nonzero Wronskian; the difference has zero Cauchy data. Writing the difference equation as a first-order system with continuous bounded coefficients on any compact radial interval, its integral equation forces zero on sufficiently short successive subintervals (the supremum norm is at most the interval length times the coefficient bound times itself), and finitely many such intervals cover every compact interval. This proves uniqueness and the asserted representation on all of $(R,\infty)$. [step 1.2, F3, F5, algebra]
