items/ex-cm-regular-lagrangians-need-not-be-hyperregular.md:63:[F2] Monotonicity and injectivity ([[thm-monotonicity-from-the-derivative]], [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]], [[def-invertible-euclidean-linear-map]], [[def-linear-map]]): since $\mathcal L_1''>0$ on $\mathbb R$, the function $\varphi_1:=\mathcal L_1'$ is strictly increasing, hence injective; since $\mathcal L_2'\equiv0$ is constant, it is not injective; since $\varphi_3=\mathrm{id}$ is the identity, it is bijective with inverse itself.
items/thm-cm-euler-equations-and-torque-free-invariants.md:111:4.1 The two functions of clause 2 are constant: the product rule and symmetry of $I^{-1}$ in [L7] give $\frac{d}{dt}\langle l,l\rangle=2\langle l',l\rangle=2\langle l\times(I^{-1}l),l\rangle$ and $\frac{d}{dt}\tfrac12\langle l,I^{-1}l\rangle=\langle l',I^{-1}l\rangle=\langle l\times(I^{-1}l),I^{-1}l\rangle$. Both inner products vanish because a cross product is orthogonal to each of its factors [L5]. Also $\frac{d}{dt}\tfrac12\langle l,I^{-1}l\rangle=\frac{d}{dt}\tfrac12\omega^{\mathsf T}I\omega$ because $I^{-1}l=\omega$ and $I$ is symmetric [L4], [L6]. These functions are continuous on the solution interval $J$ and differentiable at every interior point, so their zero derivatives make them constant by [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]. [step 3.1, given, L4, L5, L6, L7, algebra]
items/pthm-em-guide-completeness.md:86:2.1 [Clause (c), first-order modal equation] Let $J$ be an open time interval and let a strong first-order solution obey the hypotheses of the Statement: $E\in C^1(J;H)\cap C(J;V)$, $B\in C^1(J;L^2)\cap C(J;H(\operatorname{curl}))$, with the first-order equations holding as $L^2$ identities and both distributional divergence constraints, the magnetic normal-zero condition $\int_\Omega B(t)\cdot\nabla\psi=0$ for every smooth scalar $\psi$ on the closure, and $E(t)\in V$ encoding the electric tangential-zero data; no magnetic membership in $X$ is assumed. Put $a_j(t):=\langle E(t),u_j\rangle$. To pair $\operatorname{curl}B$ against the smooth eigenfield $u_j$, use the collar cutoff of [F4]: write $s$ for the inward normal coordinate in a tubular neighbourhood of the compact boundary, so that $|n\times u_j|\le Cs$ in the collar because $u_j$ is smooth on the closure with $n\times u_j=0$, and let $\eta_\delta$ be the standard smooth step with $\eta_\delta=0$ for $s\le\delta$ and $1$ for $s\ge2\delta$. Then $\eta_\delta u_j\to u_j$ and $\operatorname{curl}(\eta_\delta u_j)\to\operatorname{curl}u_j$ in $L^2$ as $\delta\to0$, since the derivative-cutoff term is supported on a layer of volume $O(\delta)$ and is bounded by $C/\delta$ times $O(s)$; the distributional identity of [F4] on the compactly supported smooth test $\eta_\delta u_j$ gives $\langle\operatorname{curl}B,\eta_\delta u_j\rangle=\langle B,\operatorname{curl}(\eta_\delta u_j)\rangle$, and Cauchy–Schwarz with the two limits gives $\langle\operatorname{curl}B,u_j\rangle=\langle B,\operatorname{curl}u_j\rangle$ for the actual graph-regular field $B$. Hence $a_j'=\langle E',u_j\rangle=v_{\mathrm{wave}}^2\langle\operatorname{curl}B,u_j\rangle=v_{\mathrm{wave}}^2\langle B,\operatorname{curl}u_j\rangle$ on $J$. The assumed $B\in C^1(J;L^2)$ permits one further derivative: $a_j''=v_{\mathrm{wave}}^2\langle B',\operatorname{curl}u_j\rangle=-v_{\mathrm{wave}}^2\langle\operatorname{curl}E,\operatorname{curl}u_j\rangle=-v_{\mathrm{wave}}^2\lambda_j\langle E,u_j\rangle$, where the last equality is the full-$X$ eigenvariational identity of [F3] tested at $v=E(t)\in V\subset X$; thus $a_j''+v_{\mathrm{wave}}^2\lambda_ja_j=0$, and $a_j$ is $C^2$ because $E$ and $B$ are $C^1$ in their norms. If $\lambda_j=0$, the same identity at $v=u_j$ gives $\|\operatorname{curl}u_j\|_2^2=0$, so $\operatorname{curl}u_j=0$ and $a_j'=v_{\mathrm{wave}}^2\langle B,\operatorname{curl}u_j\rangle=0$: the electric zero modes are constant under first-order Maxwell evolution, while the unrestricted second-order wave of step 1.3 retains its affine zero modes. This distinguishes first-order Maxwell data from arbitrary second-order $V\times H$ data; the pairing imposed no magnetic electric-type trace, and the divergence constraints and the declared weak magnetic normal condition remain part of the model. [step 1.3, F3, F4]
items/pthm-cm11-torque-free-invariants.md:171:[L3] The balance is $L_c'=0$ on $I_0$, $L_c$ is differentiable, and a continuous real function on an interval with vanishing derivative at every interior point is constant ([[post-cm11-torque-balance]] clauses (iii)-(iv), [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]).
items/pthm-cm11-torque-free-invariants.md:189:1.2 By [L3] the continuous function $L_c$ has zero derivative on $I_0$, so it is constant on $I_0$; hence $\lVert L_c\rVert_2$ is constant, and $\lVert L_c(t)\rVert_2^{2}=\langle l(t),l(t)\rangle=C$ by [L1]. This is clause 1. [given, L1, L2, L3]
items/lem-lorentz-exponential-map-and-rapidity.md:123:[L9] For a real function on an order-convex interval: sums, products and scalar multiples of differentiable functions are differentiable with the usual derivative formulas, and a function continuous on the interval whose derivative vanishes at every interior point is constant ([[def-derivative]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]).
items/prop-gr-fx-memory-integral.md:83:on this tube, in the exact sense of [[def-gr-flat-linearized-perturbation]], with the planar first-order coefficient $h_{AB}=H_{AB}(x^0-x^3)$ above and all other $h_{ab}=0$. The normalized remainder $k$ is smooth jointly in $(\lambda,x)$, and $\lambda$ is a dimensionless amplitude parameter. For small $|\lambda|$, use the future cone containing $\partial_0$ on a smaller tube. Issue the timelike geodesic $\gamma_\lambda$ from $\gamma_0(u_1)$ with unit initial velocity $\partial_0/\sqrt{-g_\lambda(\partial_0,\partial_0)}$. Choose a smooth initial orthonormal spatial frame tending to $(\partial_1,\partial_2,\partial_3)$ at $\lambda=0$, and parallel-transport it along $\gamma_\lambda$. Let $J_\lambda$ be the exact Jacobi field with initial value $s_0^AE_{A,\lambda}(u_1)$ and covariant initial derivative zero, and define $s_\lambda^A(u):=g_\lambda(J_\lambda,E_{A,\lambda})$, $A=1,2$. These are screen components in the transported frame; initial rest is covariant rest, not a coordinate-velocity condition. The geodesics, frames and Jacobi fields exist on the full fixed interval for sufficiently small $|\lambda|$, and $J_\lambda$ is realizable as a smooth timelike geodesic variation. There are constants for this fixed tube, interval and normalized family such that
items/prop-gr-fx-memory-integral.md:136:1.2 In the adapted frame of [A2], the plane-wave curvature supplier gives $R^{(1)A}{}_{00B}=\tfrac12\partial_u^2H^A{}_B$ ([F3]); the Jacobi equation of [F4] with initial screen value $s_0^Ae_A$ and zero initial derivative therefore has this forcing at first order, since the flat background curvature is zero and the basis is constant. Thus its formal first-order response obeys exactly the defining equation for $\delta s^A$ in the Statement. No finite-amplitude error estimate is used. [A2, F2, F3, F4, given, algebra]
