items/def-sr-affine-phase-and-wave-covector.md:75:is then the affine hyperplane $p_0+\ker k$ through any of its points $p_0$, and the level set is empty when $t$ is not a value of $\theta$. If $k=0$ then $\theta$ is constant and $\Sigma_t$ is either empty or all of $A$. For the derivative assertion, $I\subseteq\mathbb R$ is an order-convex set with at least two points, and a **$C^1$ curve** $\gamma:I\to A$ means a map whose four real components in an inertial chart are differentiable with continuous derivatives, using one-sided derivatives at included endpoints. Its tangent $\gamma'(s)$ is the vector with those component derivatives in the frame basis; equivalently it is the limit in frame coordinates of $(\gamma(s+h)-\gamma(s))/h$. This definition and tangent are independent of the inertial chart: chart transitions are affine with invertible constant linear part, and finite linear combinations preserve derivatives and their continuity by [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]] and [[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]]. Finally, for every such $C^1$ curve $\gamma:I\to A$ one has $(\theta\circ\gamma)'(s)=k\bigl(\gamma'(s)\bigr)$ for all $s\in I$: in the coordinates of any inertial chart this is the identity $\frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm ds}\bigl(k_ax^a(\gamma(s))+\theta(o)\bigr)=\sum_ak_a\frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm ds}x^a(\gamma(s))$. The sum and scalar-multiple rules of [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], iterated over the four components, and the zero difference quotient of the constant $\theta(o)$ justify this equality, including one-sided endpoints. Its right-hand side is the same linear combination read in the coordinates of $\gamma'(s)$.
