items/lem-fd-local-balance-equivalence.md:108:1.1 Fix $V$ and $t_0\in I$. Its compact boundary is covered by finitely many relatively compact $C^1$ graph charts. On smaller compact chart pieces the graph functions have bounded derivative. Cover each two-dimensional base by squares of side $\delta$; the graph over each square has Euclidean diameter at most $C\delta$, hence lies in an axis-parallel cube of side $C'\delta$. There are $O(\delta^{-2})$ such squares, so the total cube volume is $O(\delta)$ and tends to zero. Thus for every positive bound the boundary has a finite axis-parallel box cover of smaller total volume, so $K=\overline V$ is compact Jordan in the sense of [L1]. The map $\chi_{t_0}$ is a diffeomorphism on a neighborhood of $K$, so $\chi_{t_0}(K)=\overline{P_{t_0}}$ and $\partial P_{t_0}=\chi_{t_0}(\partial V)$. Its derivative is bounded on finitely many compact chart neighborhoods of $\partial V$, so it is Lipschitz there. The image of each graph-covering square has diameter at most a constant times $\delta$, so it lies in an axis-parallel cube with volume $O(\delta^3)$; summing over $O(\delta^{-2})$ squares gives arbitrarily small finite cube covers of $\partial P_{t_0}$. Thus $\chi_{t_0}(K)$ is also compact Jordan. In particular, the volume integral over $P_{t_0}$ equals the corresponding integral over $\chi_{t_0}(K)$ for every continuous integrand because their difference is on the volume-null boundary. The fields are $C^1$ on neighborhoods of the swept closures. Pulling a common such neighborhood back by the continuous map $(t,a)\mapsto(t,\chi(t,a))$ gives an open set containing $\{t_0\}\times K$; compactness supplies an interval around $t_0$ and an open reference neighborhood $U_0$ of $K$ on whose product the fields are defined. Restrict the motion to that neighborhood and interval, with reference body $V$ and reference domain $U_0$, translate time so that $t_0$ is zero, and apply [L1] to $K$ and each component of $f=\rho u$. The transport formula gives $\frac{d}{dt}\int_{P_t}\rho u\,dx=\int_{P_t}\left(\partial_t(\rho u)+\operatorname{div}(\rho u\otimes u)\right)dx$ at $t_0$. Componentwise product differentiation and [A1] give $\partial_t(\rho u)+\operatorname{div}(\rho u\otimes u)=\rho D_tu$. The linear-momentum balance [A2], traction representation [F1], and divergence theorem [F2] now give $\int_{P_{t_0}}(\rho D_tu-\rho b-\operatorname{div}\sigma)\,dx=0$. Every sufficiently small open spatial ball $Q$ with compact closure in $\Omega_{t_0}$ is a material image $P_{t_0}$: its inverse image under $\chi_{t_0}$ is a bounded $C^1$ domain compactly contained in $B$. If a component of this continuous residual were positive or negative at an interior point, it would retain that strict sign on a small ball and have a nonzero integral, a contradiction. Hence $\rho D_tu=\rho b+\operatorname{div}\sigma$ pointwise. [given, L1, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, A1, A2]
