items/cex-cm-dalembert-and-vakonomic-evolutions-differ.md:73:[F3] Calculus: the chain rule and the power rule give $\frac{d}{dt}(1+t^2)^{-1/2}=-t(1+t^2)^{-3/2}$ and $\frac{d}{dt}\bigl[t(1+t^2)^{-1/2}\bigr]=(1+t^2)^{-3/2}$ for all real $t$ ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-derivative]], [[def-abs-value]]); a differentiable function on an interval with vanishing derivative is constant ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]), and the Euclidean inner product is that of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]] ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[def-interval]]).
items/thm-csm-examples-flow-fine-entropy.md:57:2.1 For fixed $t$, around each point choose a source chart and a smaller coordinate neighborhood whose closure maps under $\chi_t$ into a target chart. Continuity and compactness give finitely many closed coordinate boxes $Q_j$ with interiors covering the torus and with each $\chi_t(Q_j)$ inside a target chart. Put $E_1=Q_1$ and $E_j=Q_j\setminus\bigcup_{i<j}\operatorname{int}(Q_i)$; each $E_j$ is compact, the pieces cover the torus, and any overlap lies on one of the box faces. Each $E_j$ is Jordan because its boundary lies in a finite union of coordinate faces, which are compact $C^1$ hypersurface patches. A $C^1$ graph patch has bounded slope on a compact subpatch, so its mesh-$h$ box cover has total $n$-volume $O(h)$ and hence content zero. On compact chart neighborhoods $\chi_t$ has bounded derivative, so it maps these zero-content boundaries to zero-content boundaries. Each compact piece and its image therefore have Jordan boundaries and lie in source and target charts; [F2] applies there. Summing over the pieces, overlaps do not affect the integrals, translation chart changes preserve $da$, and the constant $c$ is the same on both sides. Thus for every continuous $\varphi$ on the torus, $\int\varphi\circ\chi_t\,d\mu=\int\varphi J_t\,d\mu=\int\varphi\,d\mu$. [given, F1, F2, step 1.1]
items/prop-gr-fx-s1-loop-noncontractible.md:108:1.1 The map $f(x,y)=x^2+y^2$ has derivative $(2x,2y)$, which is nonzero on $f^{-1}(1)$; thus $1$ is a regular value and [F2] makes $S^1$ a smooth one-dimensional submanifold. Let $\widetilde\alpha=x\,dy-y\,dx$ be the ambient one-form on $\mathbb R^2$, let $\iota:S^1\hookrightarrow\mathbb R^2$ be inclusion, and put $\alpha=\iota^*\widetilde\alpha$, which is defined by [F8] and smooth by [F9]. The global tangent field $X=(-y,x)|_{S^1}$ spans each tangent line and satisfies $\alpha(X)=x^2+y^2=1$. By [F10], $d\alpha$ is a smooth two-form. By [F4], $d\alpha(X,X)=X(\alpha(X))-X(\alpha(X))-\alpha([X,X])=0$; since $X$ spans each one-dimensional tangent space, this gives $d\alpha=0$ intrinsically on $S^1$. Along $\gamma$, $\dot\gamma(t)=2\pi X_{\gamma(t)}$, so [F6] gives $\int_\gamma\alpha=\int_0^1 2\pi\,dt=2\pi$; the constant loop has integral zero. [given, F2, F3, F8, F9, F4, F6, F10, algebra]
