items/thm-gr-killing-and-on-shell-matter-identities.md:141:2.1 (Contraction along geodesics.) Let $\gamma$ be a geodesic with $D_t\gamma'=0$ and $\xi$ Killing. By [L3], $\frac{d}{dt}g(\xi,\gamma')=g(D_t\xi,\gamma')+g(\xi,D_t\gamma')=g(D_t\xi,\gamma')$, and $D_t\xi$ is the restriction to $\gamma$ of $\nabla_{\gamma'}\xi$. By (a), $g(\nabla_{\gamma'}\xi,\gamma')=\tfrac12(\nabla_a\xi_b+\nabla_b\xi_a)\gamma'^a\gamma'^b=0$. Hence the derivative vanishes identically and the function is constant on $I$; this proves (b). [step 1.1, L3, algebra]
items/ex-cm-averaging-oscillatory-deviation-check.md:63:1.1 By [L4], the continuous field $f(t,x)=\cos t$ is $2\pi$-periodic and bounded by one; its state derivative is zero, so $M=1$ and $L=0$ meet the hypotheses on $O=\mathbb R$. By [L1], $\bar f=0$ and $g(t,x)=\sin t$, so the averaged system is $y'=0$; the constant curve $y\equiv0$ is its solution with $y(0)=0$ on $[0,100]$, and $\|y(t)-0\|=0\le R-\rho=\tfrac12$. The constants are $M=1$, $L=0$, $B=4\pi$, $B_1=0$, $K=e^{0}[4\pi+0]=4\pi$, and with $\varepsilon=\tfrac1{100}$, [L3] gives $\varepsilon K=\tfrac{\pi}{25}<\tfrac4{25}<1/2=\rho$. [given, L1, L3, L4, algebra]
items/lem-emg-classical-charged-scalar-compact-variation.md:97:1.1 Fix the compact support $K$ of the directions. Use [F2]'s finite smooth localization near $K$, independently of the variation parameter. In each chart the localized density difference and its parameter derivative are continuous on a fixed compact parameter-rectangle product: every density is a smooth algebraic expression in the nondegenerate metric, its derivatives through order two, and the matter fields through order one. The families in [F1] have the required joint derivatives. The one-variable fundamental theorem gives the difference quotient as the average of those parameter derivatives along the parameter segment. Uniform continuity on this compact product makes that quotient converge uniformly to the derivative at zero. Monotonicity and the absolute-value estimate [F6], compared with the constant $\sup_Q|f|$, give $|\int_Q f|\le\operatorname{vol}(Q)\sup_Q|f|$; this passes the limit through each finite Riemann integral. Thus the relative actions can be differentiated by differentiating their densities, even when the background global actions diverge. [given, F1, F2, F3, F5, F6, algebra]
