items/pthm-cm08-time-energy.md:97:[F5] Calculus and dimensions ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[def-interval]], [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]], [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]], [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]], [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-cm01-units]]): $E$ is of the stated regularity along motions; a $C^{1}$ function on the interval $J_{\max}$ whose derivative vanishes everywhere is constant, and a function that is constant on $J_{\max}$ has zero derivative at every point of $J_{\max}$; all quantities carry their declared dimensions.
items/thm-nrqm-orbital-and-spin-symmetric-power-su2-representation.md:199:1.1 [The zero tensor power] For $n=0$, the conventions give $P_+=I$, $\mathcal S_0=\mathbb C v_0$, $U_0(W)=I$, and $J_i=0$. Clauses (a)-(c) follow immediately; all ladder and Casimir formulas in (d) vanish, and $v_0\mapsto e_0$ identifies the zero operators with the spin-zero model [F10]. Each $V_i(t)=I$ has derivative zero and is strongly continuous, and $U_0$ is constant, proving (e)-(f); the subgroup and $A_i$ assertions, independent of $n$, are proved below. For the tensor and basis calculations in the remaining steps assume $n\ge1$. [given, F10, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-microcausal-star-product.md:92:4.1 If $K$ is smooth, multiplying $F^{(2)}(\phi)$ by $K$ is allowed by the smooth-factor case of [F3] and adds no wavefront directions. Pairing the result against the cutoff metric density $\chi\mu_g^{\otimes2}$ is defined because the distribution has compact support. For each output derivative, the corresponding wavefront covectors of $F$ have zero entries in the two contracted slots; if the output had an all-future or all-past nonzero covector, adjoining those zeros would put $F^{(s+2)}(\phi)$ in a forbidden cone from [F1]. Hence $\Gamma_KF$ is microcausal. Its degree is at most $\deg F-2$ because two functional derivative slots were contracted; constants and degree-one functionals give zero. [F1, F3, given, algebra] ∎
items/pthm-cm03-momentum-balance.md:78:[L3] A continuous real function on an interval whose derivative vanishes at every interior point is constant ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]). Differentiability, sums and scalar multiples of curves behave as in [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]; finite sums are linear ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]]).
