items/ex-cm06-rolling-disk-model-comparison.md:137:[F4] Calculus and equivalence: on a nondegenerate interval $J$, a $C^1$ curve $q=(x,\theta)$ has $\frac{d}{dt}(x-a\theta)=x'-a\theta'$. Thus $x'-a\theta'=0$ on $J$ holds if and only if $x-a\theta$ is constant on $J$. For any fixed $t_0\in J$, the zero-level constraint $\mathcal G(q(t))=0$ for all $t\in J$ is equivalent to that velocity law together with $\mathcal G(q(t_0))=0$; the velocity law alone permits any initial constant level ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]], [[def-interval]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]).
