items/pthm-cm08-point-noether.md:99:[F6] Calculus along the motion ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[def-interval]], [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]): the curves $t\mapsto\xi(t,x(t))$ and $t\mapsto B(t,x(t))$ are $C^{1}$ with derivatives $D_{t}\xi(t,x(t),x'(t))$ and $D_{t}B(t,x(t),x'(t))$; sums and pairings of differentiable curves differentiate by the product rule; a $C^{1}$ real function on an interval is constant if and only if its derivative vanishes at every point.
items/pthm-cm08-point-noether.md:113:2.2 Clause 2, conservation criterion: by step 1.2 the derivative of $t\mapsto C(t,x(t),x'(t))$ is the pairing displayed there; by [F6] a $C^{1}$ function on the interval $J$ is constant on $J$ if and only if its derivative vanishes everywhere on $J$, so $C$ is constant on $J$ exactly when the pairing vanishes for every $t\in J$. [F6, step 1.2]
items/thm-fd-x09.md:71:2.1 The fundamental theorem of calculus and the chain rule give $\nabla h(\rho)=(P'(\rho)/\rho)\nabla\rho=\rho^{-1}\nabla(P(\rho))$. For a steady solution, $u_t=0$, so dotting the momentum equation with $u$ and applying step 1.1 yields $u\cdot\nabla(|u|^2/2+h(\rho)+V)=0$, because $u\cdot(u\times\operatorname{curl}u)=0$. Along a streamline $x'(s)=u(x(s))$, the chain rule makes the derivative of this quantity zero, proving streamline constancy. If $\operatorname{curl}u=0$, step 1.1 and the momentum equation give $\nabla(|u|^2/2+h(\rho)+V)=0$. A differentiable function with zero gradient is constant along every polygonal path; in an open connected subset of $\mathbb R^3$, polygonally reachable points form a nonempty relatively open and relatively closed set (each point has a ball contained in the open set). Connectedness therefore gives constancy on that component. [given, F1, step 1.1, algebra]
