items/cor-cm-noether-charges-for-translations-rotations-and-boosts.md:62:[F2] The cross product is alternating and bilinear, and $(a\times u)\cdot w=a\cdot(u\times w)$ for all $a,u,w\in\mathbb R^3$ ([[def-cross-product-in-r3]], [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]); in particular $v\cdot(a\times v)=0$ and $\frac{d}{dt}(a\times q)=a\times q'$ along a differentiable curve ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[def-interval]], [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]).
items/ex-nrqm-oscillator-formal-nonnormalizable-solution.md:69:(d) every $C^2$ function $u:\mathbb R\to\mathbb C$ (with complex smoothness and derivatives understood componentwise as in [[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]]) that belongs to $L^2(\mathbb R,dy)$ and solves $u''=(y^2-1)u$ is a constant multiple of $g_0$: the quantity $W(u):=g_0u'-g_0'u$ is constant for such $u$; if this constant is nonzero then $u(y)=g_0(y)\bigl(C+c\pi^{1/2}F(y)\bigr)$ for some constants with $c\ne0$, which satisfies $|u(y)|\ge K e^{y^2/2-2y+1}$ for some $K>0$ and all sufficiently large $y$, and is not in $L^2$; hence the constant is zero and $u/g_0$ is constant.
