items/thm-sr-fermi-walker-and-rigid-observer-examples.md:100:1.1 Preservation (a): write $s$ for the given parameter and $\tau'(s)>0$ for its proper-time derivative. By [F1], $X'=\tau'c^{-2}(\eta(A,X)U-\eta(U,X)A)$ and likewise for $Y$. Bilinearity gives $\frac{d}{ds}\eta(X,Y)=\eta(X',Y)+\eta(X,Y')=\tau'c^{-2}[\eta(A,X)\eta(U,Y)-\eta(U,X)\eta(A,Y)+\eta(A,Y)\eta(U,X)-\eta(U,Y)\eta(A,X)]=0$. The two pairs cancel, so the scalar pairing is constant on the interval by [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]; the fields are $C^1$, including continuity at any included endpoints. Norms and orthogonality are therefore preserved. For the unique transported fields with prescribed tetrad initial values, all pairings remain $\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)_{ab}$, and the time leg is $U/c$ because [F1] makes $U/c$ transported with the same initial value. Hence the family remains an observer-adapted tetrad. [F1, F7, given]
items/lem-em-rectangle-mode-profile-identities.md:75:1.2 [One-dimensional orthogonality and norms] Let $m,m'\ge0$ and $p,p'\ge1$ be integers. By [F6], $\cos(m\pi x/L)\cos(m'\pi x/L)=\frac12\bigl[\cos\bigl(\frac{(m-m')\pi x}{L}\bigr)+\cos\bigl(\frac{(m+m')\pi x}{L}\bigr)\bigr]$, so the second fundamental theorem [F3] applied to the continuous antiderivatives, together with the integer values [F5], gives $\int_0^L\cos\frac{m\pi x}{L}\cos\frac{m'\pi x}{L}\,dx=0$ for $m\ne m'$, $=\frac{L}{2}$ for $m=m'\ge1$ and $=L$ for $m=m'=0$; uniformly $\int_0^L\cos\frac{m\pi x}{L}\cos\frac{m'\pi x}{L}\,dx=\delta_{mm'}\frac{L}{2}(1+\delta_{m0})$. Likewise $\int_0^L\sin\frac{p\pi x}{L}\sin\frac{p'\pi x}{L}\,dx=\frac{L}{2}\delta_{pp'}$, because $\sin\frac{p\pi x}{L}\sin\frac{p'\pi x}{L}=\frac12\bigl[\cos\frac{(p-p')\pi x}{L}-\cos\frac{(p+p')\pi x}{L}\bigr]$ and every nonzero integer multiple of $\pi$ is a zero of sine (and the case $p=p'$ leaves the constant $1$ in the first term). [F3, F5, F6, algebra]
items/ex-emg-ml-b04.md:55:1.1 Take the candidate expression of [F2], $C=mg(U,\xi)+qA(\xi)=mU_0+qA_0$, with $\xi=\partial_0$ and $\chi=0$. The actual $C^2$ potential suffices for the following direct calculation. In Cartesian Minkowski coordinates the connection coefficients vanish; by [F1], the supplied $C^2$ curve obeys the Lorentz law, whose lowered time component is $m\,dU_0/d\tau=qF_{0i}U^i$. Since $A_0=-\phi/c$, $A_i=0$ and $\partial_0\phi=0$, exterior differentiation gives $F_{0i}=-\partial_iA_0=\partial_i\phi/c$. The chain rule therefore yields $dC/d\tau=qF_{0i}U^i+q\partial_iA_0U^i=0$ at every interior parameter value. A real differentiable function with zero derivative is constant on each compact interior subinterval by the mean-value theorem; continuity extends the same value to the included endpoints of the finite segment. This proves constancy at the stated $C^2$ regularity without a smooth-potential application of [F2]. [given, F1, F2, A1, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-orbital-and-spin-legendre-rodrigues-equation.md:85:1.3 [(c), Legendre's equation] If $l=0$, then $P_0=1$ and the equation is immediate. Suppose $l\ge1$. Differentiating $F_l=(t^2-1)^l$ with [F1] gives $F_l'=2lt(t^2-1)^{l-1}$, hence $(t^2-1)F_l'=2ltF_l$. Apply $\frac{d^{l+1}}{dt^{l+1}}$ to both sides using [F2]; on the left the third and higher derivatives of $t^2-1$ vanish, so $(t^2-1)F_l^{(l+2)}+(l+1)(2t)F_l^{(l+1)}+\binom{l+1}{2}(2)F_l^{(l)}$, and on the right $2l\bigl(tF_l^{(l+1)}+(l+1)F_l^{(l)}\bigr)$, where $\binom{l+1}{2}=\frac{l(l+1)}2$ by [F3]; subtracting gives $(t^2-1)F_l^{(l+2)}+2tF_l^{(l+1)}-l(l+1)F_l^{(l)}=0$. Since $P_l=\frac1{2^ll!}F_l^{(l)}$ is exactly the $l$-fold derivative, dividing by the nonzero constant and multiplying by $-1$ yields $(1-t^2)P_l''-2tP_l'+l(l+1)P_l=0$ at every real $t$: the displayed polynomial identity holds for the polynomial $p_l$ by [F4]. [given, F1, F2, F3, F4, algebra]
items/lem-gr-flrw-metric-and-comoving-data.md:136:[L5] The maximal integral curves of a smooth vector field through the points of its domain exist and are unique ([[thm-unique-maximal-integral-curve-through-each-point]], [[def-integral-curve-of-a-vector-field]]); a smooth vector field is a smooth section of the tangent bundle ([[def-smooth-vector-field-as-a-tangent-bundle-section]]). A differentiable real function on an interval with zero derivative is constant ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]), so solving $dt/ds=c^{-1}$ and zero spatial velocity gives the displayed integral curves in product charts.
