items/lem-sr-dust-conservation-equivalence.md:66:3.1 Clause (c). Let $x:J\to\Omega$ be a $C^2$ curve with $x'(s)=n(x(s))$. By [F3], $x''^b(s)=\partial_an^b(x(s))\,x'^a(s)=n^a\partial_an^b(x(s))$, which vanishes under the conservation hypothesis by step 2.1 (or, in the nonnegative-density branch, at every $s$ with $x(s)\in\{\rho>0\}$ by step 2.1 applied there). Hence every component $(x^b)'$ has zero derivative at every interior point of $J$, and [F2] gives that $(x^b)'$ is constant; applying [F2] to the function $s\mapsto x^b(s)-x^b(s_0)-(x^b)'(s_0)(s-s_0)$, whose derivative vanishes, gives $x^b(s)=x^b(s_0)+(x^b)'(s_0)(s-s_0)$ for all $s\in J$ and every $b$. Thus $x$ is an affine straight line and $x''=0$ on $J$. [F2, F3, step 2.1]
items/pthm-tgr-perfect-entropy-circulation.md:64:[F7] [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]] (published mathematical corollary): a continuous real function on an order-convex interval whose derivative vanishes at every interior point is constant on the interval. The local circulation function is differentiable on its flow interval, so this applies when its derivative vanishes throughout that interval.
items/lem-nrqmx-cubic-moyal-commutator-remainder.md:115:4.1 The kernel $K_{\epsilon,a}(q,r)$ is compactly supported in $q$ and Schwartz in $r$, since $a\in C_c^\infty$. The formula derived in step 3.1, applied also to each $\partial_q^\alpha V$, and the bounded derivatives of $V$ show that $\Delta_V(q,r)$ and all its $q,r$ derivatives grow at most polynomially in $r$, uniformly for $q$ in that compact set. Hence $G(q,r):=\Delta_V(q,r)K_{\epsilon,a}(q,r)$ is Schwartz in $(q,r)$; its partial Fourier transform is Schwartz by [F3], and Fourier inversion [F3] recovers $G$. Define the remainder symbol by the inverse partial Fourier transform $$ r_{\epsilon,a,V}(q,\eta):=\frac{i}{\epsilon^3}\int e^{-ir\cdot\eta/\epsilon}\Delta_V(q,r)K_{\epsilon,a}(q,r)\,dr. $$ By construction its Weyl kernel is $i\epsilon^{-3}\Delta_V(q,r)K_{\epsilon,a}(q,r)$, so the remaining potential commutator kernel, after multiplication by $i/\epsilon$, is $\epsilon^2$ times that kernel. Apply [F8] to the positive scaling $s\mapsto r=\epsilon s$ in this absolutely integrable complex Fourier integral. This gives $$ r_{\epsilon,a,V}(q,\eta)=i(2\pi)^{-d}\int e^{-is\cdot\eta}\frac{\Delta_V(q,\epsilon s)}{\epsilon^3}\left(\int e^{is\cdot\xi}a(q,\xi)\,d\xi\right)ds. $$ For $|\alpha|\le N_d$, the formula from step 3.1 applied to $\partial_q^\alpha V$ gives $|\partial_q^\alpha\Delta_V(q,\epsilon s)|\le C_{\alpha,V}\epsilon^3|s|^3$, since every third derivative of $\partial_q^\alpha V$ is bounded by hypothesis. Derivatives in $\eta$ insert powers of $s$; derivatives in $q$ of the partial Fourier transform insert derivatives of $a$. Repeated coordinatewise integration by parts in $\xi$, justified by [F7], gives decay factor $(1+|s|^2)^{-4d-5}$ uniformly in $q$, with zero boundary terms because $a$ is compactly supported in $\xi$. For $|\alpha|\le N_d$, the largest remaining radial power is $|s|^{N_d+3}=|s|^{4d+7}$; after the $d$-dimensional radial measure, the tail is bounded by a constant times $\int_1^\infty\rho^{-3d-4}d\rho<\infty$. More explicitly, bounding all $q,\eta$ derivatives of total order at most $N_d$ uses $(1-\Delta_\xi)^{4d+5}$ on $a$, hence at most $N_d+8d+10=12d+14$ derivatives, within $M_d=12d+16$. This proves the stated uniform derivative bound. [step 3.1, given, A1, F3, F6, F7, F8, algebra]
items/pthm-em-charge-continuity.md:129:1.2 In the distributional branch, fix $\psi\in C_c^\infty(\Omega\times I)$. The distributional curl is built from the components $\partial_jB_k-\partial_kB_j$, so $\langle\operatorname{div}(\operatorname{curl}B),\psi\rangle=\sum_{i,j,k}\varepsilon_{ijk}\langle\partial_i\partial_jB_k,\psi\rangle$, where $i,j,k$ range over $1,2,3$ and $\varepsilon_{ijk}$ is $+1$ for cyclic permutations of $(1,2,3)$, $-1$ for the other permutations and $0$ for repeated indices; the distributional derivatives commute and $\varepsilon_{ijk}$ is antisymmetric, so exchanging the summation indices $i$ and $j$ presents this number as its own negative, and hence it is zero. The Ampere identity in $\mathcal D'$, linearity of the pairing and commutativity with $\partial_t$ give $0=\mu_0\langle\operatorname{div}J,\psi\rangle+c^{-2}\langle\partial_t\operatorname{div}E,\psi\rangle$; the Gauss identity in $\mathcal D'$ reads $\operatorname{div}E=\rho/\varepsilon_0$, so $\partial_t\operatorname{div}E=\varepsilon_0^{-1}\partial_t\rho$ as distributions, and substitution with $c^2\varepsilon_0\mu_0=1$ and $\mu_0>0$ gives $\langle\partial_t\rho+\operatorname{div}J,\psi\rangle=0$. As $\psi$ was arbitrary, $\partial_t\rho+\operatorname{div}J=0$ in $\mathcal D'(\Omega\times I)$; every ingredient is either a distributional identity or a constant multiple, so no choice principle is used. [F4, given, algebra]
