items/ex-cm06-pendulum-tension-and-reaction-power.md:128:[F6] Energy of a one-label curve family ([[def-cm03-centre-of-mass-and-momenta]] clauses (b),(d)): $K=\tfrac12m(x'^{2}+y'^{2}+z'^{2})$ and $E=K+U(t,x(t),y(t),z(t))$; the Euclidean pairing ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]]); a differentiable function with zero derivative on an interval is constant ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]], [[def-interval]]).
items/ex-em-tem-topology-example.md:58:(c) [Coaxial example] For the following Lebesgue polar-coordinate energy calculation assume Countable Choice $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $0<a<b$, $A:=\{x\in\mathbb R^2:a<|x|<b\}$ (two boundary components), fix $V\in\mathbb R$, choose the forward label $k:=\omega/v$, and define $\varphi(x):=V\ln(b/|x|)/\ln(b/a)$, so that $\tilde E=V e_r/(|x|\ln(b/a))$ with $e_r:=x/|x|$ and $\tilde H=(V/(Z|x|\ln(b/a)))e_\varphi$ with $e_\varphi:=e_z\times e_r$. Then $\Delta\varphi=0$ on $A$: indeed $\partial_i\ln|x|=x_i/|x|^2$ by [[thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral]] and the chain rule ([[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]]), and the second partial derivatives sum to zero; hence $\varphi$ is constant on each boundary circle (values $V$ at $|x|=a$ and $0$ at $|x|=b$), the fields satisfy the source-free lossless macroscopic equations and the PEC conditions, and the relation $k=\omega/v$ is nondispersive with no cutoff. The amplitude integrals computed in polar coordinates with [[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]] (the Jacobian factor $r$ is read from that formula) are $$W_e:=\tfrac12\varepsilon\int_A|\tilde E|^2\,dA=\tfrac12CV^2,\qquad W_m:=\tfrac12\mu\int_A|\tilde H|^2\,dA=\tfrac12CV^2,\qquad C:=\frac{2\pi\varepsilon}{\ln(b/a)};$$ the instantaneous per-length energies are $\tfrac12CV^2\cos^2\theta$ and $\tfrac12LI^2\cos^2\theta$ with $\theta=kx_3-\omega t$; equivalently, with the per-length inductance $L:=\mu\ln(b/a)/(2\pi)$ in $\mathrm H\,\mathrm m^{-1}$, per-length capacitance $C$ in $\mathrm F\,\mathrm m^{-1}$, and wall-circulation amplitude $I:=2\pi V/(Z\ln(b/a))$ in $\mathrm A$ (identified with the inner-conductor sheet-current amplitude when its conductor-side magnetic intensity trace is zero) one has $W_m=\tfrac12LI^2$ and $LC=\varepsilon\mu$. For $V\ne0$ the two boundary components carry distinct constants, allowing a nonzero field; $V=0$ gives equal constants and the zero field.
items/ex-em-tem-topology-example.md:87:2.1 [Single-conductor obstruction] Suppose $\varphi\in C^2(\overline A;\mathbb C)$ is harmonic on the bounded connected domain $A$ and has constant boundary trace on each connected component of $\partial A$, with the PEC potential description of step 1.1 available. If $\partial A$ is connected, the trace is a single constant $c$, and applying [F4] to the real and imaginary parts of $\varphi$ and of the constant function $c$ gives $\varphi=c$ on $\overline A$; then $\tilde E=-\nabla\varphi=0$ and $\tilde H\propto e_z\times\tilde E=0$. For a rectangular cross-section with a potential in $C^2(A)\cap C(\overline A)$ having constant boundary trace the same argument applies, because [F4] requires only a bounded nonempty open set and no boundary regularity. For rectangular separated TEM amplitudes that are $C^1$ on the interior and continuous on the closure with the classical PEC traces on the open edges, fix a star centre $x_*$ and use the radial primitive $\psi(x)=\int_0^1\tilde E(x_*+t(x-x_*))\cdot(x-x_*)\,dt$ furnished by [F5]. It is $C^2$ on the interior with $\nabla\psi=\tilde E$; the displayed formula extends continuously to the closure by uniform continuity of the electric amplitude there. For two points on one open edge, apply the interior FTC line-integral identity on parallel interior segments and pass to the edge using this continuity. Thus the boundary potential increment is the integral of the continuous tangential electric trace, whose difference quotients give the edge derivative; PEC makes that derivative zero; by the one-variable constancy of [F6] the potential is constant on each open edge, and continuity at the corners joins those constants, so the trace is constant on $\partial A$ and [F4] gives $\psi$ constant, hence $\tilde E=0$ and $\tilde H=0$. Thus a single-conductor cross-section with connected boundary admits no nonzero TEM field in the stated potential form, and the same holds for the stated rectangular class. [step 1.1, F1, F2, F4, F5, F6]
