items/thm-fd-rectangular-no-slip-stokes-velocity-pressure.md:87:where $\mathscr R'$ is the same construction on $\Omega'$ using $\theta_2,\ldots,\theta_d$. Smoothness and differentiation under these finite integrals follow from the smooth zero extension of the tests to a neighborhood of the closed box. Fubini gives $\int_{\Omega'}H=\int_\Omega h=0$. The first-component derivative is $h-\theta_1H$; the sum of the other divergence terms is $\theta_1H$ by induction, proving $\operatorname{div}(\mathscr Rh)=h$. If $h$ is supported in a fixed compact $K$, choose closed intervals inside each side containing its coordinate projection and $\operatorname{supp}\theta_i$. Their product contains every output support: the first primitive vanishes before this interval and after it because its full first-coordinate integral is zero, and the other components have this support by induction. For $p_m$ the supremum of derivatives of order at most $m$, $p_m(H)\le(b_1-a_1)p_m(h)$. Differentiating the displayed formula bounds each output derivative by a fixed bump-derivative constant times $p_m(h)$, a finite-integral length times that bound, or $p_m(\mathscr R'H)$. The one-dimensional primitive has $p_m(\mathscr Rh)\le\max(1,b_1-a_1)p_m(h)$; induction therefore gives $p_m(\mathscr Rh)\le C_{\Omega,m}p_m(h)$. All operations are linear. This proves that this chosen map has the right-inverse and support-continuity properties, without extracting a quantitative Sobolev estimate from [F4]. [given, F4, step 1.2, construct, algebra, induction]
