items/lem-cm-second-order-oscillator-form.md:59:[L3] A function continuous on $I$ whose derivative vanishes at every interior point of $I$ is constant on $I$ ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]).
items/thm-nrqm-intervals-finite-well-root-conditions-and-bound-count.md:85:1.2 [Energies $E\le-U$ are excluded] Let $H\psi=E\psi$ with $\|\psi\|_{L^2}=1$ and $E\le-U$. By [F3], $E=\langle H\psi,\psi\rangle =\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\|\psi'\|_2^2+\int_{[-a,a]}V|\psi|^2$ and this is at least $-U$. Thus $E=-U$ and equality holds in the lower bound, so $\|\psi'\|_2=0$. The absolutely continuous representative of an $H^1$ function with derivative zero is constant on every bounded interval by [F2], hence is a single constant on $\mathbb R$; the only constant in $L^2(\mathbb R)$ is $0$. This contradicts $\|\psi\|_2=1$, so $E>-U$. [F2, F3, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-intervals-finite-well-root-conditions-and-bound-count.md:87:1.3 [Parity and matching] Let $E=-\varepsilon\in(-U,0)$, $\kappa=\sqrt{2m\varepsilon}/\hbar$, $k=\sqrt{2m(U-\varepsilon)}/\hbar$, so $k^2+\kappa^2=K^2$. On an exterior interval the classical equation is $\psi''=\kappa^2\psi$. Put $g=\psi'-\kappa\psi$; then $g'=-\kappa g$, so $(e^{\kappa x}g)'=0$ and $g=Ce^{-\kappa x}$. Integrating $(e^{-\kappa x}\psi)'=Ce^{-2\kappa x}$ shows that every solution is $A e^{\kappa x}+B e^{-\kappa x}$. The $L^2$ condition removes the growing term on each exterior half-line, leaving multiples of $e^{-\kappa x}$ on $x>a$ and $e^{\kappa x}$ on $x<-a$. On $(-a,a)$ the equation is $\psi''=-k^2\psi$. The matrix mapping $(A,B)$ to the value and derivative of $A\cos(kx)+B\sin(kx)$ at any $x_0$ has determinant $k\ne0$, so choose $A,B$ to match the data of any solution there. The difference $w$ solves $w''=-k^2w$ with zero data at $x_0$; differentiating $|w'|^2+k^2|w|^2$ shows this quantity is constant, hence $w=0$. Thus every interior solution is $A\cos(kx)+B\sin(kx)$. The potential is even, so the reflection operator $(\Pi\psi)(x)=\psi(-x)$ commutes with $H$ on $H^2$ and maps every eigenspace to itself. Eigenspaces are at most one-dimensional: the solutions decaying as $x\to-\infty$ form a one-dimensional space $c\,e^{\kappa x}$ ($x<-a$), and the transfer through $(-a,a)$ is invertible by the just-proved initial-data representation, so at most one direction matches the decaying solution at $+\infty$. Hence $\Pi\psi=\pm\psi$: every eigenfunction is even or odd. For an even eigenfunction, $\psi(x)=A\cos(kx)$ on $(-a,a)$ and $\psi(x)=Be^{-\kappa|x|}$ for $|x|>a$; matching $\psi$ and $\psi'$ at $a$ gives $A\cos(ka)=Be^{-\kappa a},\qquad -Ak\sin(ka)=-\kappa Be^{-\kappa a},$ which eliminates $A,B$ and gives $k\tan(ka)=\kappa$. For an odd eigenfunction, $\psi(x)=A\sin(kx)$ on $(-a,a)$ and $\psi(x)=B\operatorname{sgn}(x)e^{-\kappa|x|}$ outside; matching gives $A\sin(ka)=Be^{-\kappa a}$ and $Ak\cos(ka)=-\kappa Be^{-\kappa a}$, i.e. $-k\cot(ka)=\kappa$. Conversely, if either condition holds with $k,\kappa>0$, $k^2+\kappa^2=K^2$, then the indicated piecewise function is $C^1$, piecewise $C^2$, satisfies $H\psi=E\psi$ on each constant-potential interval and decays exponentially. Its restrictions and their first derivatives are square-integrable, and its piecewise classical second derivative is square-integrable because the central interval is bounded and the exterior pieces decay exponentially. To verify the global weak derivatives, integrate against a compactly supported smooth test function separately on $(-\infty,-a)$, $(-a,a)$ and $(a,\infty)$: the boundary terms cancel at $\pm a$ for the first derivative because $\psi$ is continuous, and for the second derivative because $\psi'$ matches there. Thus the piecewise first and second derivatives are the global weak derivatives and belong to $L^2(\mathbb R)$, so $\psi\in H^2(\mathbb R)$ by [[def-nrqm-intervals-hilbert-sobolev-spaces]]. It is nonzero and therefore an eigenfunction. This proves (b). [F2, algebra]
