items/pthm-cm06-rheonomic-power.md:114:[L5] Bilinearity of the Euclidean product and linearity of finite sums ([[def-euclidean-inner-product]], [[lem-finite-sum-laws]]); a continuous function on a closed bounded interval is Riemann integrable ([[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]), and the Newton-Leibniz formula integrates an integrable derivative when the primitive is continuous on the closed interval and differentiable in its interior ([[thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative]], [[def-interval]], [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]); a differentiable function on an interval with zero derivative is constant ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]).
items/pthm-cm06-rheonomic-power.md:138:- **Conservation is conditional and representation-dependent.** Clause 3 gives sufficient conditions: a scleronomic constraint, a static potential and vanishing residual forces along the motion. They are not necessary; by clause 2 and the zero-derivative criterion, energy is constant exactly when its displayed total rate vanishes along the motion. For example, with one label, $O=\mathbb R\times\mathbb R^3$, $G(t,x)=x_1-vt$ for a constant nonzero speed $v$, $F=0$, $U=0$ and $x(t)=(vt,0,0)$, the regular constraint is rheonomic, but $R=0$, $\lambda=0$ and $E=mv^2/2$ is constant. More generally the three rate terms can cancel. The normalization of $U$ by a time-dependent additive function changes $E$ and adds $c'(t)$ to the right-hand side of clause 2, exactly as in [[def-cm03-potential-and-mechanical-energy]] clause (c).
items/lem-emg-sphere-flux-holonomy.md:158:2.3 Let $u:M_0\to U(1)$ be any smooth gauge map and restrict it to $\gamma(\phi)=(x^0_*,e^{i\phi},y_*,z_*)$, with the other base coordinates fixed; write $v(\phi)=u(\gamma(\phi))$. The chain rule [F10] and smooth group inversion [F3] make $v$ and $v^{-1}$ smooth. Since $|v|=1$, differentiating $\bar v v=1$ gives $\overline{v^{-1}v'}+v^{-1}v'=0$, so $q(\phi):=-i v^{-1}v'$ is real and smooth. It is continuous on $[0,2\pi]$, hence integrable by [F7]. By [F12] and [F13], choose $\chi_0\in[0,2\pi)$ with $e^{i\chi_0}=v(0)$, and set $\chi(\phi)=\chi_0+\int_0^\phi q(s)\,ds$; [F8] gives $\chi'=q$. By [F10] and [F14], $h=e^{i\chi}$ satisfies $h'=iqh$, while the definition of $q$ gives $v'=iqv$. Thus $(vh^{-1})'=0$; applying [F9] to its real and imaginary parts, whose zero derivatives are integrable by [F7], yields $vh^{-1}=v(0)h(0)^{-1}=1$, so $v=e^{i\chi}$ on the full interval. Any other smooth phase lift $\widetilde\chi$ of $v$ has the same derivative as $\chi$ by [F10] and [F14]; applying [F9] to $\widetilde\chi-\chi$ shows the difference is constant. Since $v(2\pi)=v(0)$, $e^{i(\chi(2\pi)-\chi(0))}=1$, and [F11] gives $\chi(2\pi)-\chi(0)=2\pi n$ for some integer $n$, independently of the phase lift. Since this argument uses only a smooth circle-valued loop and its equal endpoint values, it proves the same phase-lift and winding claims for every smooth $g:S^1\to U(1)$. Hence $\int_\gamma(-iu^{-1}du)=2\pi n$. [given, F3, F7, F8, F9, F10, F11, F12, F13, F14, step 1.2, algebra]
