items/thm-emx-matrix-energy-constraints.md:84:[F5] A function continuous on an interval and differentiable with zero derivative at every interior point is constant on that interval ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]).
items/thm-emx-matrix-energy-constraints.md:96:2.1 Define $z(t):=e^{(t-t_0)A}(z_0+H(t))$, where $z_0=(e_0,b_0)$. By [F2] and step 1.2, the product rule gives $z'=Az+f(t)$, since $e^{(t-t_0)A}e^{-(t-t_0)A}=I$ by the commuting-product identity in [F2]; also $z(t_0)=z_0$. Thus a $C^1$ solution exists on all of $I$. If $n_e+n_b=0$, every state is the unique empty vector, so the solution is unique. Otherwise, if $\widetilde z$ is another solution with the same initial value, then $\xi(t):=e^{-(t-t_0)A}(\widetilde z(t)-z(t))$ has derivative zero by [F2] and the two differential equations. Each coordinate of $\xi$ is constant by [F5] and vanishes at $t_0$, so $\widetilde z=z$. [given, F2, F5, step 1.2, algebra]
items/ex-cm-rolling-disk-acceleration.md:89:[F5] If a differentiable function on an interval has identically vanishing derivative, it is constant ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]], [[def-interval]]).
items/ex-cm-adiabatic-drift-check.md:92:[L7] The zero-derivative constancy fact used below is [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]: a differentiable real function on an interval whose derivative vanishes is constant.
items/pthm-fd-local-periodic-incompressible-model-evolution.md:120:For $d=1,2$, the lift defined in the Statement has zero derivatives in the omitted coordinates and zero omitted velocity components. Its divergence is $\nabla_d\cdot u$, its convective acceleration has first $d$ components $(u\cdot\nabla_d)u$ and remaining components zero, and its three-dimensional stress divergence has first $d$ components $\nabla_d\cdot\sigma^{(d)}$ and remaining components zero. Applying the same constant-viscosity identity in three dimensions therefore gives $\nabla_d\cdot(2\mu D_du)=\mu\Delta_du$ when $\nabla_d\cdot u=0$. In every case the adopted reduced momentum equation is $\partial_tu+(u\cdot\nabla_d)u=-\rho_*^{-1}\nabla_dp+(\mu/\rho_*)\Delta_du$. [given, A1, A2, A3, algebra]
items/thm-tgr-supported-static-heat-evolution.md:150:3.1 For every compact time interval in $t_K>0$, the integrands and their time derivatives are continuous on its product with $\overline\Omega$; the fundamental theorem of calculus in time and [F11] therefore justify differentiating the finite-volume integral. The heat balance and boundary condition give $$\frac{d}{dt_K}\int_\Omega N\epsilon\,dx=\int_\Omega NC_V\partial_{t_K}T\,dx=-\int_\Omega\operatorname{div}_x(N^2q)\,dx=0.$$ The last equality follows by applying the one-dimensional fundamental theorem of calculus to each coordinate derivative and summing the six face fluxes, each zero by the insulated condition. Continuity at $t_K=0$ extends this constant value to the initial time, proving conservation of $\mathcal E_K$. [step 2.3, step 2.1, F11, algebra]
items/thm-tgr-supported-static-heat-evolution.md:154:4.2 The same compact-time differentiation argument as in step 3.1 and the heat equation give $\frac{d}{dt_K}\int_\Omega\vartheta\,dx=(A_0/C_V)\int_\Omega\Delta\vartheta\,dx=0$. The last integral vanishes by the coordinate fundamental theorem of calculus on each pair of opposite faces, whose normal derivatives are zero. Continuity to the initial trace then shows that the mean of $\vartheta(t_K,\cdot)$ is the positive constant $\overline\vartheta$. [step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1, A2, F11, algebra]
items/ex-cm17-periodic-shear-exact-solution-check.md:120:3.1 Periodicity: since $p$ is $1$-periodic in $x_1$, step 1.2 at $x_1=1$ gives $0=\nu v''(x_2)$ for every $x_2$, and $\nu>0$ gives $v''\equiv0$. Apply [F3] to $v'$ on any compact subinterval of its real periodic representative: all increments vanish, so $v'=\alpha$ is constant. A second application gives $v(x_2)=\alpha x_2+\beta$; $v(1)=v(0)$ forces $\alpha=0$. Conversely, constant $v$ with constant zero-mean pressure satisfies the equations by zero derivatives. Thus the steady periodic viscous shear profiles are exactly the constants. [step 1.2, F3, algebra] ∎
