items/thm-nrqm-intervals-barrier-transfer-and-thick-limit-error.md:84:1.1 [Shape of bounded solutions] On either exterior interval the equation is $\psi''=-k^2\psi$. At any point $x_0$, the matrix sending $(A,B)$ to the value and derivative of $A\cos(kx)+B\sin(kx)$ has determinant $k\ne0$, so choose $A,B$ to match any solution's data there. The difference $w$ then solves $w''=-k^2w$ with $w(x_0)=w'(x_0)=0$; differentiating $|w'|^2+k^2|w|^2$ and using [F1] shows it is constant, hence zero, so every solution is $A\cos(kx)+B\sin(kx)$ and is bounded on each half-line. On $(0,a)$ let $v$ solve $v''=\kappa^2v$ and put $g:=v'-\kappa v$. Then $g'=-\kappa g$, so $(e^{\kappa x}g)'=0$ and $g=Ce^{-\kappa x}$. Solving $(e^{-\kappa x}v)'=Ce^{-2\kappa x}$ gives $v=Ae^{\kappa x}+Be^{-\kappa x}$. Thus each interval has a two-dimensional solution space; a function satisfying the regularity in the statement is described by two constants on each interval, with no boundedness restriction on the exterior coefficients. [F1, F2, algebra]
items/ex-em-variational-mathematical-prerequisites-hypothesis-boundaries.md:45:Two exact boundary checks. (i) Stationarity is first order. On $\mathbb R^2$ the polynomial $S(x,y)=x^2-y^2$ has differential $DS(0,0):\mathbb R^2\to\mathbb R$ equal to the zero map at $(0,0)$ in the total-derivative sense of [[def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space]], while $S(\varepsilon,0)=\varepsilon^2>0$ and $S(0,\varepsilon)=-\varepsilon^2<0$ for every $\varepsilon\ne0$; a point where the first variation vanishes need not be a minimum. Consequently the equivalence of [[lem-em-compact-field-variation]] between stationarity and the coefficient equation, and the conversion by [[lem-em-fundamental-variation-lemma]] of a vanishing first variation into pointwise equations, are first-order conditions: they carry no minimality and no existence content, and the example involves no adopted physical action. (ii) Compact support of the variation is essential. Assume $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]) for this Lebesgue-functional branch, exactly as in the compact-field supplier; the polynomial branch (i) remains choice-free. Take $U=(-1,1)^4$, $\kappa=1$, $J=0$ and the smooth field $A$ with $A_0(x)=(x^1)^2$ and $A_1=A_2=A_3=0$, in the conventions of [[def-em-minkowski-affine-space]]. Then $F_{01}=-2x^1$, $F_{10}=2x^1$, $F^{01}=\eta^{00}\eta^{11}F_{01}=2x^1$ and $F^{10}=-2x^1$, with every remaining component vanishing, so $\partial_\mu F^{\mu0}=\partial_1F^{10}=-2\ne0$ and the coefficient equation $\partial_\mu F^{\mu\nu}=-\kappa J^\nu$ fails at $\nu=0$. Nevertheless the variation $h$ with $h_0\equiv1$ and $h_1=h_2=h_3=0$ is smooth but not compactly supported in $U$; along it $A_0+sh_0=(x^1)^2+s$, so $F_{01}$ and the integrand $L=2(x^1)^2$ are independent of $s$ and $S[A+sh]=\int_U2(x^1)^2\,d\lambda$ is constant in $s$, with first variation $0$ although the field equation fails. For the complementary stationarity-boundary check take instead $A_0=x^1$, the other covariant components zero, $J=0$, and $h_0=x^1$, $h_i=0$ (so $A^0=-x^1$, $h^0=-x^1$). Here $F_{01}=-1$ is constant, so the coefficient equations all hold, but this smooth noncompact direction gives $S[A+sh]=8(1+s)^2$ and derivative $16\ne0$ at zero. Thus extending the compact stationarity condition to all smooth noncompact directions would invalidate the converse. These two witnesses show that the stated compact-support hypothesis cannot simply be removed: its coefficient conversion has no boundary term precisely in its declared compact class. Other variation classes require their own boundary hypotheses.
