## Definition

Let \(M\) be an oriented smooth manifold of finite dimension \(n\), with a chosen integration cycle and a smooth positive volume form \(\mu\). Its parity-reversed tangent bundle \(\Pi TM\) has even base coordinates \(x^1,\ldots,x^n\) and odd fiber coordinates \(\theta^1,\ldots,\theta^n\), which transform as the coordinate one-forms \(dx^i\). Smooth functions on \(\Pi TM\) are identified with inhomogeneous complex differential forms \(\mathcal A=\Omega^\bullet(M;\mathbb C)\); the parity of a form is its degree modulo two. The Berezin convention is \(\int d\theta^n\cdots d\theta^1\,\theta^1\cdots\theta^n=1\). For \(F\in\mathcal A\), write \(F_{[n]}\) for its degree-\(n\) component and define the graded integral by \(I_\Gamma(F):=\int_M F_{[n]}\), equivalently by Berezin integration over the odd fibers followed by integration over the body \(M\) with the chosen orientation.

A supercharge \(Q\) is a smooth odd derivation of \(\mathcal A\), so for homogeneous forms \(\alpha,\beta\) it satisfies \(Q(\alpha\wedge\beta)=Q\alpha\wedge\beta+(-1)^{|\alpha|}\alpha\wedge Q\beta\). Its square \(Q^2:=Q\circ Q\) is an even derivation. The measure and cycle are \(Q\)-invariant on an admissible class of integrands when \(I_\Gamma(QF)=0\) for every \(F\) in that class. In a coordinate description this is the zero-divergence condition \(\operatorname{div}_{\mu}Q=0\), where \(\mathcal L_Q\mu=(\operatorname{div}_{\mu}Q)\mu\), together with vanishing boundary flux. Compact support in the interior or explicit decay may impose the boundary condition on a noncompact or bounded cycle.

An admissible finite-dimensional \(Q\)-exact deformation consists of even smooth forms \(S,O\in\mathcal A\), an odd smooth form \(V\in\mathcal A\), and an open interval \(J\subseteq\mathbb R\) such that \(QS=QO=0\). The even form \(QV\) is the \(Q\)-exact deformation, \(S_t:=S+tQV\), and \(E_t:=e^{-S_t}\), with the exponential defined by the usual pointwise exponential of its degree-zero part and the finite exterior-degree expansion of its positive-degree part. Require \((Q^2V)E_t=0\) for every \(t\in J\); the stronger condition \(Q^2V=0\) is sufficient. Require \(O E_t\) and \(O V E_t\) to be admissible integrands, with no boundary flux in \(I_\Gamma(Q(O V E_t))\).

Write \((O E_t)_{[n]}=f_t\mu\). The deformation data are analytically admissible when \(f_t\) is integrable for every \(t\in J\), \(t\mapsto f_t(x)\) is differentiable for almost every \(x\), \(\partial_t f_t\) is measurable, and for every compact \(K\subset J\) there is a single nonnegative \(g_K\in L^1(M,\mu)\) such that \(|f_t(x)|\le g_K(x)\) and \(|\partial_t f_t(x)|\le g_K(x)\) for every \(t\in K\) outside one \(\mu\)-null set. The underlying manifold is finite-dimensional; its smooth-function algebra need not be finite-dimensional as a vector space.

## Definition references

[Schwarz and Zaboronsky, §§2–3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/9511112)

[Pestun and Zabzine, §1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1608.02953)

[Folland, Theorem 2.27](https://djvu.online/file/NPF4BEtSuqdFA)

