## Statement

Let \(M\) be an oriented smooth manifold of finite dimension \(n\), with integration cycle \(M\), smooth positive volume form \(\mu\), graded algebra \(\mathcal A=\Omega^\bullet(M;\mathbb C)\), and integral \(I_\Gamma(F)=\int_M F_{[n]}\) of the degree-\(n\) part. Let \(Q\) be a smooth odd derivation of \(\mathcal A\), let \(S,O\) be smooth even forms, let \(V\) be a smooth odd form, and let \(J\subseteq\mathbb R\) be an open interval. Suppose \(QS=QO=0\), \((Q^2V)e^{-S-tQV}=0\) for every \(t\in J\), and \(I_\Gamma(Q(O V e^{-S-tQV}))=0\) for every \(t\in J\), including the zero-boundary-flux condition. Write \((Oe^{-S-tQV})_{[n]}=f_t\mu\). Suppose that \(f_t\) is integrable for each \(t\in J\), is differentiable in \(t\) almost everywhere with measurable derivative, and that for every compact \(K\subset J\) one integrable majorant \(g_K\) bounds both \(|f_t|\) and \(|\partial_t f_t|\) for all \(t\in K\), outside one null set. Then

$$Z(t):=I_\Gamma(Oe^{-S-tQV})=\int_M f_t\,d\mu$$

is constant on \(J\), and for every \(t\in J\),

$$Z'(t)=-I_\Gamma(O(QV)e^{-S-tQV})=-I_\Gamma(Q(O V e^{-S-tQV}))=0.$$

In particular, the conclusion is finite-dimensional and applies only at finite parameter values in \(J\); it gives no \(t\to\infty\) saddle-point limit and no identity for an infinite-dimensional continuum functional integral.

## Statement references

[Schwarz and Zaboronsky, §§2–3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/9511112)

[Pestun and Zabzine, §1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1608.02953)

[Folland, Theorem 2.27(b)](https://djvu.online/file/NPF4BEtSuqdFA)

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** \(M,\mu,\mathcal A,I_\Gamma,Q,S,O,V,J\) satisfying the Statement, with \(Q\)-invariant integration on each \(O V e^{-S-tQV}\), the stated \(Q^2V\) condition, and the compact-parameter integrable majorants.

[F1] [[def-finite-dimensional-q-exact-localization-data]]: \(\mathcal A=\Omega^\bullet(M;\mathbb C)\), \(I_\Gamma(F)=\int_M F_{[n]}\), \(Q\) is an odd derivation with the graded Leibniz rule, and \(Q\)-invariance means \(I_\Gamma(QF)=0\) for the admissible integrands, with boundary flux excluded.

[F2] [[thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign]]: for a measurable family \(f(x,t)\) whose sections are integrable, whose parameter derivative exists almost everywhere and is measurable, and whose derivative has one \(L^1\) majorant uniform on an open parameter interval, the integral \(F(t)=\int f(x,t)\,d\mu(x)\) is differentiable and \(F'(t)=\int \partial_t f(x,t)\,d\mu(x)\).

[F3] [[def-derivative]]: for a real-valued function \(u\) at an interior point \(t\), \(u'(t)\) is the finite limit of \((u(t+h)-u(t))/h\) as \(h\to0\), \(h\ne0\).

[F4] [[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]: a real-valued function continuous on an interval and differentiable at every interior point with derivative zero throughout is constant on that interval.

## Proof

1.1 Put \(E_t=e^{-S-tQV}\). Since \(QV\) is even, coefficientwise differentiation of its exponential gives \(\partial_tE_t=-(QV)E_t\). The top-degree projection is linear and \(O\) is independent of \(t\), so \(\partial_t f_t=-[O(QV)E_t]_{[n]}\). [given, F1, algebra]

1.2 Fix \(t_0\in J\) and choose \(a<t_0<b\) with \([a,b]\subset J\). The majorant \(g_{[a,b]}\) makes every \(f_t\) integrable on \((a,b)\) and bounds its measurable parameter derivative there by one \(L^1(M,\mu)\) function. Applying [F2] on \((a,b)\), and using step 1.1, gives \(Z'(t_0)=-I_\Gamma(O(QV)E_{t_0})\). [given, F2, step 1.1]

2.1 The assumptions \(QS=0\) and \((Q^2V)E_t=0\) give \(QE_t=-(QS+tQ^2V)E_t=0\). The graded Leibniz rule, \(QO=0\), and the odd parity of \(V\) then give \(Q(OV E_t)=(QO)VE_t+O(QV)E_t-OV(QE_t)=O(QV)E_t\). [given, F1, algebra]

2.2 Substituting step 2.1 into the derivative formula of step 1.2 yields \(Z'(t_0)=-I_\Gamma(Q(OV E_{t_0}))=0\), because the cycle and measure have zero \(Q\)-boundary contribution. Since \(t_0\) was arbitrary, \(Z'\) vanishes throughout \(J\). [given, F1, step 1.2, step 2.1]

3.1 Write \(Z=u+iv\) with real functions \(u,v\). Step 2.2 implies that both real difference quotients have limit zero at every \(t\in J\). By [F3], for each \(t\) and all sufficiently small nonzero \(h\), \(|u(t+h)-u(t)|<|h|\) and \(|v(t+h)-v(t)|<|h|\); hence \(u\) and \(v\) are continuous at every \(t\). Applying [F4] to each component shows that both are constant on \(J\). Therefore \(Z\) is constant on \(J\). [F3, F4, step 2.2] ∎

